window.MOLASTRIA_RXN = {"index":{"title":"Reaction Kinetics & Transition States","sub":"From a saddle point on the energy surface to how many turnovers a catalyst makes per second","eyebrow":"MOLASTRIA · REACTION KINETICS","h1":"Reaction Kinetics &amp; Transition States","question":"Raise a barrier by just 5.7 kJ/mol and the reaction runs ten times slower. So is the highest barrier always the step that sets the rate?","lead":"Chemical reactions happen on potential energy surfaces: reactants and products are valleys, and the transition state is the mountain pass between them. This section follows one thread through eight chapters: first find the pass on the surface (minima, saddles, NEB, the climbing image, IRC); then turn its height into a rate constant with transition state theory (the Eyring equation); then see how unimolecular reactions depend on pressure (RRKM), how light atoms pass through barriers (tunnelling) and how rates change with isotopes (KIE); and finally combine many elementary steps into a catalytic cycle and compute its turnover frequency (microkinetics and the energetic span model). All thirteen demos are computed live in the browser, and several are checked digit by digit against independent exact solutions.","scope":"<b>How to read the labels.</b> Every chapter and key point carries a status: history, theorem (derived rigorously from statistical mechanics, quantum mechanics or algebra), established by experiment (supported by a large body of experiment and computation, in everyday use), approximation (holds under the stated conditions), frontier (still changing fast) and open. Demos carry one of two labels: exact (computed live from a formula or numerical solution, with assumptions listed under each demo) and toy model (a minimal model built to show one thing clearly, not a real system). The surface is the Müller–Brown model potential and RRKM uses a clearly labelled “toy molecule”, neither of which is data for a real molecule; the Eyring equation, the Eckart transmission probability and the steady state of catalytic cycles are exact.","levels":{"history":["History","Documented history and facts about the literature"],"proved":["Theorem","Derived rigorously from statistical mechanics, quantum mechanics or algebra"],"tested":["Established","Supported by a large body of experiment and computation, in everyday use"],"approx":["Approximation","Holds under the stated conditions and fails outside them"],"frontier":["Frontier","Developing fast; methods and numbers are still changing"],"open":["Open","Not yet achieved or explained by anyone"]},"demoKinds":{"exact":["Exact","Computed live from a formula or numerical solution (assumptions are listed under each demo)"],"toy":["Toy model","A minimal model built to show one thing clearly; not a real system"]},"groups":[{"title":"Surfaces and transition states","ids":["pes","paths"]},{"title":"Rate theory","ids":["tst","rrkm"]},{"title":"Quantum effects and isotopes","ids":["tunnel","kie"]},{"title":"From elementary steps to catalytic cycles","ids":["micro","span"]}],"chapters":[{"id":"pes","n":"01","title":"Potential energy surfaces: where reactions happen","summary":"In the Born–Oppenheimer approximation the nuclei move on a “landscape” made of the electronic energy. Minima are reactants, products and intermediates; first-order saddle points are transition states, curving downhill in exactly one direction. The Hammond postulate, the Bell–Evans–Polanyi relation and Marcus’s two parabolas are all rules read off the shape of this map.","level":"proved","demos":["pes","hammond"]},{"id":"paths","n":"02","title":"Finding transition states: NEB, climbing image, dimer and IRC","summary":"Finding a minimum only needs downhill steps, but a saddle needs uphill in one direction and downhill in all others. The nudged elastic band (NEB) links a chain of “images” with springs so that the chain settles onto the minimum energy path; the climbing image (CI-NEB) then pushes the highest image up to the saddle. The dimer method climbs along the softest direction using gradients alone. Once a saddle is found, the IRC walks downhill from it in both directions to confirm that it really connects the reactants and products you intended.","level":"tested","demos":["neb","irc"]},{"id":"tst","n":"03","title":"Transition state theory and the Eyring equation","summary":"In 1935 Eyring, and independently Evans and Polanyi, arrived at the same formula: the rate constant equals a universal frequency k_BT/h times e^{−ΔG‡/RT}. It turns kinetics into the “thermodynamics of the transition state”: know the free-energy barrier and you can estimate the rate. At room temperature every 5.7 kJ/mol of barrier is a factor of ten in rate. The formula rests on a few assumptions, above all no recrossing; recrossing, solvent friction and tunnelling all go into the transmission coefficient κ.","level":"approx","demos":["eyring","plot"]},{"id":"rrkm","n":"04","title":"Unimolecular reactions: Lindemann, RRK and RRKM","summary":"For an isolated molecule to fall apart or isomerise by itself, it must first gain enough energy in collisions and then wait for that energy to flow into the bond that breaks. Lindemann’s mechanism explains why the rate constant switches from second to first order as the pressure rises; RRKM theory says that a molecule with energy E reacts at k(E) = the number of states of the transition state divided by h times the reactant’s density of states. States are counted with the Beyer–Swinehart algorithm, trillions of them in a second.","level":"approx","demos":["rrkm","falloff"]},{"id":"tunnel","n":"05","title":"Tunnelling: through the barrier, not over it","summary":"Hydrogen atoms, protons and hydrides are light enough to pass through barriers. The Wigner correction 1 + u²/24 works only at high temperature; Bell’s parabolic barrier diverges at low temperature; the Eckart barrier has an exact transmission probability that can be integrated over the whole energy distribution. At low temperature tunnelling can decide the product outright, as when methylhydroxycarbene tunnels only to acetaldehyde at 11 K.","level":"approx","demos":["eckart"]},{"id":"kie","n":"06","title":"Kinetic isotope effects","summary":"Replacing H by D leaves the potential energy surface untouched; only the mass changes. The heavier atom vibrates more slowly and has a lower zero-point energy, so it loses less zero-point energy at the transition state, and the reaction slows down. Counting only the C–H stretch, k_H/k_D at room temperature is about 6–7, the “semiclassical limit”; much larger values usually signal tunnelling, as in soybean lipoxygenase with k_H/k_D ≈ 80. Isotope effects are among the most direct experimental tools for telling which bond breaks in the rate-determining step.","level":"tested","demos":["kie"]},{"id":"micro","n":"07","title":"Microkinetic modelling: from elementary steps to turnover frequency","summary":"A catalytic reaction is made of elementary steps: adsorption, surface reaction, desorption. Writing each step’s rate constant (usually from the Eyring equation and DFT energies) into mass-action equations and solving for the steady state gives the surface coverages and the turnover frequency (TOF). The “degree of rate control” tells you which transition state or intermediate energy matters most for the rate; the Sabatier principle and the BEP relation together give the volcano plot of catalyst activity.","level":"approx","demos":["micro","volcano"]},{"id":"span","n":"08","title":"The energetic span model: a pair of states sets the TOF","summary":"To estimate how fast a catalytic cycle runs from a computed energy profile, it is not enough to look at “the highest step barrier”. Kozuch and Shaik’s energetic span model says that the TOF is set by a pair of states, the TOF-determining transition state (TDTS) and the TOF-determining intermediate (TDI), with span δE = T_TDTS − I_TDI, plus ΔGr if the TDTS comes before the TDI. It is a rewriting of the exact steady state of the cycle (Christiansen’s formula), and in the demo the two agree to the last digit.","level":"tested","demos":["span"]}],"milestones":[{"year":"1889","text":"Arrhenius’s rate law and the idea of “activation” <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-arrhenius1889\" data-ref=\"arrhenius1889\">Arrhenius 1889</a>]</span>."},{"year":"1918","text":"Langmuir’s adsorption isotherm (<a href=\"#c-micro\" data-open=\"micro\">07 · Microkinetic modelling: from elementary steps to turnover frequency</a>) <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-langmuir1918\" data-ref=\"langmuir1918\">Langmuir 1918</a>]</span>."},{"year":"1922","text":"Lindemann explains the pressure dependence of unimolecular reactions (<a href=\"#c-rrkm\" data-open=\"rrkm\">04 · Unimolecular reactions: Lindemann, RRK and RRKM</a>) <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-lindemann1922\" data-ref=\"lindemann1922\">Lindemann 1922</a>]</span>."},{"year":"1930","text":"The exact transmission probability of the Eckart barrier (<a href=\"#c-tunnel\" data-open=\"tunnel\">05 · Tunnelling: through the barrier, not over it</a>) <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-eckart1930\" data-ref=\"eckart1930\">Eckart 1930</a>]</span>."},{"year":"1932","text":"Wigner’s quantum correction <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-wigner1932\" data-ref=\"wigner1932\">Wigner 1932</a>]</span>."},{"year":"1935","text":"Eyring, and Evans and Polanyi, propose transition state theory independently (<a href=\"#c-tst\" data-open=\"tst\">03 · Transition state theory and the Eyring equation</a>) <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-eyring1935\" data-ref=\"eyring1935\">Eyring 1935</a>; <a href=\"#ref-evans1935\" data-ref=\"evans1935\">Evans &amp; Polanyi 1935</a>]</span>; Wynne-Jones and Eyring extend it to solutions <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-wynnejones1935\" data-ref=\"wynnejones1935\">Wynne-Jones &amp; Eyring 1935</a>]</span>."},{"year":"1936","text":"Bell gives the linear relation between barrier and heat of reaction (<a href=\"#c-pes\" data-open=\"pes\">01 · Potential energy surfaces: where reactions happen</a>) <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-bell1936\" data-ref=\"bell1936\">Bell 1936</a>]</span>."},{"year":"1940","text":"Kramers’s diffusion theory: solvent friction makes κ &lt; 1 <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-kramers1940\" data-ref=\"kramers1940\">Kramers 1940</a>]</span>."},{"year":"1947","text":"Bigeleisen and Mayer’s theory of isotope effects (<a href=\"#c-kie\" data-open=\"kie\">06 · Kinetic isotope effects</a>) <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-bigeleisen1947\" data-ref=\"bigeleisen1947\">Bigeleisen &amp; Mayer 1947</a>]</span>."},{"year":"1952","text":"Marcus formulates RRKM theory <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-marcus1952\" data-ref=\"marcus1952\">Marcus 1952</a>]</span>."},{"year":"1955","text":"The Hammond postulate <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-hammond1955\" data-ref=\"hammond1955\">Hammond 1955</a>]</span>."},{"year":"1956","text":"Marcus’s theory of electron transfer <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-marcus1956\" data-ref=\"marcus1956\">Marcus 1956</a>]</span>."},{"year":"1970","text":"Fukui defines the intrinsic reaction coordinate (<a href=\"#c-paths\" data-open=\"paths\">02 · Finding transition states: NEB, climbing image, dimer and IRC</a>) <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-fukui1970\" data-ref=\"fukui1970\">Fukui 1970</a>]</span>."},{"year":"1973","text":"The Beyer–Swinehart state-counting algorithm <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-beyer1973\" data-ref=\"beyer1973\">Beyer &amp; Swinehart 1973</a>]</span>."},{"year":"1979","text":"The Müller–Brown model potential <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-muller1979\" data-ref=\"muller1979\">Müller &amp; Brown 1979</a>]</span>."},{"year":"1994","text":"The forerunner of NEB: Mills and Jónsson’s elastic band <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-mills1994\" data-ref=\"mills1994\">Mills &amp; Jónsson 1994</a>]</span>."},{"year":"2000","text":"The climbing-image CI-NEB and the improved tangent <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-henkelman2000\" data-ref=\"henkelman2000\">Henkelman et al. 2000</a>; <a href=\"#ref-henkelman2000t\" data-ref=\"henkelman2000t\">Henkelman &amp; Jónsson 2000</a>]</span>."},{"year":"2001","text":"Campbell’s degree of rate control <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-campbell2001\" data-ref=\"campbell2001\">Campbell 2001</a>]</span>."},{"year":"2002","text":"A “universal” BEP relation for surface dissociation <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-norskov2002\" data-ref=\"norskov2002\">Nørskov et al. 2002</a>]</span>; the string method <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-e2002\" data-ref=\"e2002\">E et al. 2002</a>]</span>; the huge isotope effect of soybean lipoxygenase <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-knapp2002\" data-ref=\"knapp2002\">Knapp et al. 2002</a>]</span>."},{"year":"2006","text":"Kozuch and Shaik’s energetic span model (<a href=\"#c-span\" data-open=\"span\">08 · The energetic span model: a pair of states sets the TOF</a>) <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-kozuch2006\" data-ref=\"kozuch2006\">Kozuch &amp; Shaik 2006</a>]</span>."},{"year":"2011","text":"Tunnelling control in methylhydroxycarbene <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-schreiner2011\" data-ref=\"schreiner2011\">Schreiner et al. 2011</a>]</span>; “the rate-determining step is dead, long live the rate-determining state” <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-kozuch2011b\" data-ref=\"kozuch2011b\">Kozuch &amp; Martin 2011</a>]</span>."},{"year":"2020","text":"A transition-state dataset of about twelve thousand elementary reactions <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-grambow2020\" data-ref=\"grambow2020\">Grambow et al. 2020</a>]</span>."},{"year":"2023","text":"Transition states generated directly by an equivariant diffusion model <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-duan2023\" data-ref=\"duan2023\">Duan et al. 2023</a>]</span>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What is a stationary point whose Hessian has one negative eigenvalue and all others positive?","opts":["A minimum","A first-order saddle, i.e. a transition state","A second-order saddle","A maximum"],"a":1,"why":"It curves downhill in exactly one direction: a maximum along the reaction coordinate and a minimum in all others. A vibrational analysis shows one imaginary frequency.","ch":"pes"},{"q":"By the Hammond postulate, what does the transition state of a strongly exothermic reaction resemble more?","opts":["The reactants","The products","Exactly halfway between","It does not depend on exothermicity"],"a":0,"why":"The parabolas cross at x‡ = ½ + ΔG°/(2λ); for exothermic reactions (ΔG° &lt; 0) x‡ &lt; ½, near the reactants.","ch":"pes"},{"q":"In NEB, which part of the true force is kept on each image?","opts":["The component along the path","The component perpendicular to the path","All of it","None; only spring forces act"],"a":1,"why":"The perpendicular part drops the chain into the valley; along the path only the spring force acts, spacing the images. The climbing image is the exception: no springs, tangential true force reversed.","ch":"paths"},{"q":"At 25 °C, by what factor does a first-order rate constant change when ΔG‡ rises by 5.71 kJ/mol?","opts":["1/2","1/e","1/10","1/100"],"a":2,"why":"RT ln 10 = 8.314 × 298.15 × 2.303 / 1000 = 5.71 kJ/mol.","ch":"tst"},{"q":"Why, in classical mechanics, is transition state theory an upper bound on the rate?","opts":["Because tunnelling is ignored","Because it assumes no trajectory recrosses the dividing surface, and recrossing can only reduce the net flux","Because k_BT/h is too large","Because harmonic oscillators are used"],"a":1,"why":"That is the basis of variational TST: move the dividing surface and take the minimum flux. Tunnelling is a quantum effect that can push the true rate above classical TST.","ch":"tst"},{"q":"In the Lindemann mechanism, what is the order of a unimolecular reaction at very low pressure?","opts":["Zero","First","Second (also proportional to the collision partner’s concentration)","Third"],"a":2,"why":"At low pressure activating collisions are the bottleneck: k_uni → k₀[M], so rate = k₀[M][A].","ch":"rrkm"},{"q":"What is the RRKM k(E)?","opts":["The transition state’s total number of states divided by h times the reactant density of states","The reactant’s number of states divided by the transition-state density","k_BT/h times e^{−E₀/k_BT}","The collision frequency"],"a":0,"why":"k(E) = σ N‡(E − E₀)/(h ρ(E)). The third option is canonical TST, the high-pressure limit obtained by averaging k(E) over the Boltzmann distribution.","ch":"rrkm"},{"q":"For a reaction with imaginary frequency 1500i cm⁻¹ at 300 K, what does Bell’s infinite-parabola correction give?","opts":["About 1.1","About 3","It diverges and cannot be used","Less than 1"],"a":2,"why":"u = 1.4388 × 1500/300 = 7.2 &gt; 2π, so sin(u/2) turns negative and the formula fails. The temperature is already below the crossover temperature of 343 K.","ch":"tunnel"},{"q":"If a C–H bond breaks in the rate-determining step, roughly what is k_H/k_D at 25 °C from zero-point energy alone?","opts":["1.1","About 6–7","About 30","About 80"],"a":1,"why":"Losing the whole 2900 cm⁻¹ stretch gives about 6.4. 80 is the value for soybean lipoxygenase, where tunnelling is involved.","ch":"kie"},{"q":"A catalyst surface is almost fully covered by one intermediate. Roughly what is that intermediate’s degree of rate control?","opts":["+1","0","−1","It depends on temperature and can be anything"],"a":2,"why":"Making it a little more stable poisons more sites and lowers the TOF by the same factor. The degrees of rate control of all intermediates (including empty sites) sum to −1.","ch":"micro"},{"q":"In the energetic span model, what sets the TOF?","opts":["The step with the largest single barrier","A pair of states: the TDTS and the TDI","The absolute energy of the highest transition state","The reaction free energy ΔGr itself"],"a":1,"why":"δE = T_TDTS − I_TDI (plus ΔGr if the TDTS comes first). They need not be adjacent; in chapter 8’s worked example, looking at single-step barriers overestimates the TOF about 3000-fold.","ch":"span"},{"q":"A competition experiment (H and D substrates in one flask) shows a large isotope effect. What does it show?","opts":["C–H cleavage must control the overall rate","C–H cleavage occurs in the selectivity-determining step, not necessarily the rate-determining one","The reaction must involve tunnelling","Nothing at all"],"a":1,"why":"Only separate parallel rate measurements show whether C–H cleavage affects the overall rate (Simmons and Hartwig 2012).","ch":"kie"}],"books":[{"title":"Chemical Kinetics and Dynamics","authors":"J. I. Steinfeld, J. S. Francisco, W. L. Hase (Prentice Hall, 2nd ed. 1999)","note":"The standard graduate text from phenomenological kinetics to transition state theory, RRKM and molecular reaction dynamics."},{"title":"Chemical Kinetics","authors":"K. J. Laidler (Harper & Row, 3rd ed. 1987)","note":"A classic text, especially detailed on the history of transition state theory and on reactions in solution."},{"title":"Unimolecular Reaction Dynamics: Theory and Experiments","authors":"T. Baer, W. L. Hase (Oxford University Press, 1996)","note":"A monograph on RRKM, densities of states, intramolecular energy redistribution and non-RRKM behaviour: background for chapter 4."},{"title":"Concepts of Modern Catalysis and Kinetics","authors":"I. Chorkendorff, J. W. Niemantsverdriet (Wiley-VCH, 3rd ed. 2017)","note":"Kinetics of surface catalysis, microkinetics, BEP relations and volcano plots: background for chapter 7."},{"title":"Modern Physical Organic Chemistry","authors":"E. V. Anslyn, D. A. Dougherty (University Science Books, 2006)","note":"Energy surfaces, the Hammond postulate, isotope effects and catalysis from the organic chemist’s side: background for chapters 1 and 6."},{"title":"Introduction to Computational Chemistry","authors":"F. Jensen (Wiley, 3rd ed. 2017)","note":"The computational details of stationary-point optimisation, transition-state searches, IRC and statistical thermodynamics: background for chapters 2 and 3."}],"open":[{"q":"Can all the important transition states of a complex reaction network be found reliably and automatically, without relying on chemists’ intuition?","ch":"paths"},{"q":"For reactions in solution and in enzymes, how can the missing pieces of transition state theory (recrossing, friction, non-equilibrium solvent) be computed quantitatively?","ch":"tst"},{"q":"How far do protein motions actively “drive” hydrogen tunnelling, rather than merely bringing donor and acceptor closer?","ch":"kie"},{"q":"Are there design principles for catalysts that break the linear scaling relations and get past the top of the volcano?","ch":"micro"},{"q":"With computed free energies wrong by about 10 kJ/mol, how can computed TOFs be brought within an order of magnitude?","ch":"span"}],"notes":{"map":"Each card is a chapter. The label gives the status of the bulk of the chapter; key points inside carry their own labels. Chapters you have opened are ticked (on this device only).","history":"Years are publication years.","path":"To read on: the <a href=\"../chemistry/physical/#c-kinetics\">chemical kinetics</a> chapter of the Physical Chemistry hall is the introduction to this section; the <a href=\"../chemistry/computational/#c-pes\">potential energy surfaces</a> chapter of the Computational Chemistry hall explains where the energies come from; the statistical mechanics chapter of <a href=\"../macro/#c-statmech\">Heat, Statistics and Flow</a> covers partition functions; <a href=\"../group-theory/#c-vibrations\">Group Theory</a> covers the symmetry of vibrational modes; <a href=\"../nequip/\">NequIP</a> covers machine-learned potentials that replace quantum chemistry in dynamics; the <a href=\"../code/\">Code Desk</a> writes transition-state optimisation and frequency inputs for ORCA, Gaussian, xTB and PySCF, and the <a href=\"../models/\">model interface</a> runs optimisations and frequencies online, and <a href=\"../models/?job=ts#xtb\">finds transition states and runs IRCs</a> too.","library":"54 references. Each was looked up by DOI in OpenAlex (which draws on Crossref) for title, first author, year and volume (November 2026; node tools/reaction-refs.mjs re-checks them). The author names of Müller and Brown 1979 are garbled in OpenAlex, so only its year and volume were compared."}},"refs":[{"id":"arrhenius1889","authors":"S. Arrhenius","year":1889,"title":"Über die Reaktionsgeschwindigkeit bei der Inversion von Rohrzucker durch Säuren","journal":"Zeitschrift für Physikalische Chemie","vol":"4","pages":"226–248","doi":"10.1515/zpch-1889-0416","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["tst"]},{"id":"langmuir1918","authors":"I. Langmuir","year":1918,"title":"The adsorption of gases on plane surfaces of glass, mica and platinum","journal":"Journal of the American Chemical Society","vol":"40","pages":"1361–1403","doi":"10.1021/ja02242a004","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["micro"]},{"id":"lindemann1922","authors":"F. A. Lindemann, S. Arrhenius, I. Langmuir, N. R. Dhar, J. Perrin, W. C. McC. Lewis","year":1922,"title":"Discussion on “the radiation theory of chemical action”","journal":"Transactions of the Faraday Society","vol":"17","pages":"598–606","doi":"10.1039/TF9221700598","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["rrkm"]},{"id":"eckart1930","authors":"C. Eckart","year":1930,"title":"The penetration of a potential barrier by electrons","journal":"Physical Review","vol":"35","pages":"1303–1309","doi":"10.1103/PhysRev.35.1303","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["tunnel"]},{"id":"wigner1932","authors":"E. Wigner","year":1932,"title":"On the quantum correction for thermodynamic equilibrium","journal":"Physical Review","vol":"40","pages":"749–759","doi":"10.1103/PhysRev.40.749","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["tunnel"]},{"id":"evans1935","authors":"M. G. Evans, M. Polanyi","year":1935,"title":"Some applications of the transition state method to the calculation of reaction velocities, especially in solution","journal":"Transactions of the Faraday Society","vol":"31","pages":"875–894","doi":"10.1039/tf9353100875","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["pes","tst"]},{"id":"eyring1935","authors":"H. Eyring","year":1935,"title":"The activated complex in chemical reactions","journal":"The Journal of Chemical Physics","vol":"3","pages":"107–115","doi":"10.1063/1.1749604","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["tst"]},{"id":"wynnejones1935","authors":"W. F. K. Wynne-Jones, H. Eyring","year":1935,"title":"The absolute rate of reactions in condensed phases","journal":"The Journal of Chemical Physics","vol":"3","pages":"492–502","doi":"10.1063/1.1749713","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["tst"]},{"id":"bell1936","authors":"R. P. Bell","year":1936,"title":"The theory of reactions involving proton transfers","journal":"Proceedings of the Royal Society A","vol":"154","pages":"414–429","doi":"10.1098/rspa.1936.0060","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["pes"]},{"id":"wigner1938","authors":"E. Wigner","year":1938,"title":"The transition state method","journal":"Transactions of the Faraday Society","vol":"34","pages":"29–41","doi":"10.1039/tf9383400029","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["tst"]},{"id":"kramers1940","authors":"H. A. Kramers","year":1940,"title":"Brownian motion in a field of force and the diffusion model of chemical reactions","journal":"Physica","vol":"7","pages":"284–304","doi":"10.1016/S0031-8914(40)90098-2","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["tst"]},{"id":"bigeleisen1947","authors":"J. Bigeleisen, M. G. Mayer","year":1947,"title":"Calculation of equilibrium constants for isotopic exchange reactions","journal":"The Journal of Chemical Physics","vol":"15","pages":"261–267","doi":"10.1063/1.1746492","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["kie"]},{"id":"marcus1952","authors":"R. A. Marcus","year":1952,"title":"Unimolecular dissociations and free radical recombination reactions","journal":"The Journal of Chemical Physics","vol":"20","pages":"359–364","doi":"10.1063/1.1700424","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["rrkm"]},{"id":"hammond1955","authors":"G. S. Hammond","year":1955,"title":"A correlation of reaction rates","journal":"Journal of the American Chemical Society","vol":"77","pages":"334–338","doi":"10.1021/ja01607a027","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["pes"]},{"id":"marcus1956","authors":"R. A. Marcus","year":1956,"title":"On the theory of oxidation-reduction reactions involving electron transfer. I","journal":"The Journal of Chemical Physics","vol":"24","pages":"966–978","doi":"10.1063/1.1742723","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["pes"]},{"id":"bell1959","authors":"R. P. Bell","year":1959,"title":"The tunnel effect correction for parabolic potential barriers","journal":"Transactions of the Faraday Society","vol":"55","pages":"1–4","doi":"10.1039/tf9595500001","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["tunnel"]},{"id":"westheimer1961","authors":"F. H. Westheimer","year":1961,"title":"The magnitude of the primary kinetic isotope effect for compounds of hydrogen and deuterium","journal":"Chemical Reviews","vol":"61","pages":"265–273","doi":"10.1021/cr60211a004","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["kie"]},{"id":"fukui1970","authors":"K. Fukui","year":1970,"title":"Formulation of the reaction coordinate","journal":"The Journal of Physical Chemistry","vol":"74","pages":"4161–4163","doi":"10.1021/j100717a029","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["paths"]},{"id":"beyer1973","authors":"T. Beyer, D. F. Swinehart","year":1973,"title":"Algorithm 448: number of multiply-restricted partitions","journal":"Communications of the ACM","vol":"16","pages":"379","doi":"10.1145/362248.362275","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["rrkm"]},{"id":"stein1973","authors":"S. E. Stein, B. S. Rabinovitch","year":1973,"title":"Accurate evaluation of internal energy level sums and densities including anharmonic oscillators and hindered rotors","journal":"The Journal of Chemical Physics","vol":"58","pages":"2438–2445","doi":"10.1063/1.1679522","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["rrkm"]},{"id":"miller1975","authors":"W. H. Miller","year":1975,"title":"Semiclassical limit of quantum mechanical transition state theory for nonseparable systems","journal":"The Journal of Chemical Physics","vol":"62","pages":"1899–1906","doi":"10.1063/1.430676","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["tunnel"]},{"id":"muller1979","authors":"K. Müller, L. D. Brown","year":1979,"title":"Location of saddle points and minimum energy paths by a constrained simplex optimization procedure","journal":"Theoretica Chimica Acta","vol":"53","pages":"75–93","doi":"10.1007/BF00547608","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["pes"]},{"id":"skodje1981","authors":"R. T. Skodje, D. G. Truhlar","year":1981,"title":"Parabolic tunneling calculations","journal":"The Journal of Physical Chemistry","vol":"85","pages":"624–628","doi":"10.1021/j150606a003","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["tunnel"]},{"id":"gilbert1983","authors":"R. G. Gilbert, K. Luther, J. Troe","year":1983,"title":"Theory of thermal unimolecular reactions in the fall-off range. II. Weak collision rate constants","journal":"Berichte der Bunsengesellschaft für physikalische Chemie","vol":"87","pages":"169–177","doi":"10.1002/bbpc.19830870218","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["rrkm"]},{"id":"laidler1983","authors":"K. J. Laidler, M. C. King","year":1983,"title":"Development of transition-state theory","journal":"The Journal of Physical Chemistry","vol":"87","pages":"2657–2664","doi":"10.1021/j100238a002","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["tst"]},{"id":"truhlar1984","authors":"D. G. Truhlar, B. C. Garrett","year":1984,"title":"Variational transition state theory","journal":"Annual Review of Physical Chemistry","vol":"35","pages":"159–189","doi":"10.1146/annurev.pc.35.100184.001111","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["tst","tunnel"]},{"id":"gonzalez1989","authors":"C. Gonzalez, H. B. Schlegel","year":1989,"title":"An improved algorithm for reaction path following","journal":"The Journal of Chemical Physics","vol":"90","pages":"2154–2161","doi":"10.1063/1.456010","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["paths"]},{"id":"hanggi1990","authors":"P. Hänggi, P. Talkner, M. Borkovec","year":1990,"title":"Reaction-rate theory: fifty years after Kramers","journal":"Reviews of Modern Physics","vol":"62","pages":"251–341","doi":"10.1103/RevModPhys.62.251","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["tst"]},{"id":"marcus1993","authors":"R. A. Marcus","year":1993,"title":"Electron transfer reactions in chemistry. Theory and experiment","journal":"Reviews of Modern Physics","vol":"65","pages":"599–610","doi":"10.1103/RevModPhys.65.599","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["pes"]},{"id":"mills1994","authors":"G. Mills, H. Jónsson","year":1994,"title":"Quantum and thermal effects in H2 dissociative adsorption: evaluation of free energy barriers in multidimensional quantum systems","journal":"Physical Review Letters","vol":"72","pages":"1124–1127","doi":"10.1103/PhysRevLett.72.1124","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["paths"]},{"id":"truhlar1996","authors":"D. G. Truhlar, B. C. Garrett, S. J. Klippenstein","year":1996,"title":"Current status of transition-state theory","journal":"The Journal of Physical Chemistry","vol":"100","pages":"12771–12800","doi":"10.1021/jp953748q","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["pes","tst","tunnel"]},{"id":"henkelman1999","authors":"G. Henkelman, H. Jónsson","year":1999,"title":"A dimer method for finding saddle points on high dimensional potential surfaces using only first derivatives","journal":"The Journal of Chemical Physics","vol":"111","pages":"7010–7022","doi":"10.1063/1.480097","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["paths"]},{"id":"henkelman2000","authors":"G. Henkelman, B. P. Uberuaga, H. Jónsson","year":2000,"title":"A climbing image nudged elastic band method for finding saddle points and minimum energy paths","journal":"The Journal of Chemical Physics","vol":"113","pages":"9901–9904","doi":"10.1063/1.1329672","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["paths"]},{"id":"henkelman2000t","authors":"G. Henkelman, H. Jónsson","year":2000,"title":"Improved tangent estimate in the nudged elastic band method for finding minimum energy paths and saddle points","journal":"The Journal of Chemical Physics","vol":"113","pages":"9978–9985","doi":"10.1063/1.1323224","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["paths"]},{"id":"campbell2001","authors":"C. T. Campbell","year":2001,"title":"Finding the rate-determining step in a mechanism: comparing DeDonder relations with the “degree of rate control”","journal":"Journal of Catalysis","vol":"204","pages":"520–524","doi":"10.1006/jcat.2001.3396","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["micro"]},{"id":"e2002","authors":"W. E, W. Ren, E. Vanden-Eijnden","year":2002,"title":"String method for the study of rare events","journal":"Physical Review B","vol":"66","pages":"052301","doi":"10.1103/PhysRevB.66.052301","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["paths"]},{"id":"knapp2002","authors":"M. J. Knapp, K. Rickert, J. P. Klinman","year":2002,"title":"Temperature-dependent isotope effects in soybean lipoxygenase-1: correlating hydrogen tunneling with protein dynamics","journal":"Journal of the American Chemical Society","vol":"124","pages":"3865–3874","doi":"10.1021/ja012205t","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["kie"]},{"id":"norskov2002","authors":"J. K. Nørskov, T. Bligaard, A. Logadottir, S. Bahn, L. B. Hansen, M. Bollinger, H. Bengaard, B. Hammer, Z. Sljivancanin, M. Mavrikakis, Y. Xu, S. Dahl, C. J. H. Jacobsen","year":2002,"title":"Universality in heterogeneous catalysis","journal":"Journal of Catalysis","vol":"209","pages":"275–278","doi":"10.1006/jcat.2002.3615","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["micro"]},{"id":"bligaard2004","authors":"T. Bligaard, J. K. Nørskov, S. Dahl, J. Matthiesen, C. H. Christensen, J. Sehested","year":2004,"title":"The Brønsted–Evans–Polanyi relation and the volcano curve in heterogeneous catalysis","journal":"Journal of Catalysis","vol":"224","pages":"206–217","doi":"10.1016/j.jcat.2004.02.034","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["micro"]},{"id":"pollak2005","authors":"E. Pollak, P. Talkner","year":2005,"title":"Reaction rate theory: what it was, where is it today, and where is it going?","journal":"Chaos","vol":"15","pages":"026116","doi":"10.1063/1.1858782","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["tst"]},{"id":"fernandez2006","authors":"A. Fernández-Ramos, J. A. Miller, S. J. Klippenstein, D. G. Truhlar","year":2006,"title":"Modeling the kinetics of bimolecular reactions","journal":"Chemical Reviews","vol":"106","pages":"4518–4584","doi":"10.1021/cr050205w","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["rrkm"]},{"id":"kozuch2006","authors":"S. Kozuch, S. Shaik","year":2006,"title":"A combined kinetic−quantum mechanical model for assessment of catalytic cycles: application to cross-coupling and Heck reactions","journal":"Journal of the American Chemical Society","vol":"128","pages":"3355–3365","doi":"10.1021/ja0559146","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["span"]},{"id":"ess2007","authors":"D. H. Ess, K. N. Houk","year":2007,"title":"Distortion/interaction energy control of 1,3-dipolar cycloaddition reactivity","journal":"Journal of the American Chemical Society","vol":"129","pages":"10646–10647","doi":"10.1021/ja0734086","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["pes"]},{"id":"stegelmann2009","authors":"C. Stegelmann, A. Andreasen, C. T. Campbell","year":2009,"title":"Degree of rate control: how much the energies of intermediates and transition states control rates","journal":"Journal of the American Chemical Society","vol":"131","pages":"8077–8082","doi":"10.1021/ja9000097","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["micro","span"]},{"id":"kozuch2011","authors":"S. Kozuch, S. Shaik","year":2011,"title":"How to conceptualize catalytic cycles? The energetic span model","journal":"Accounts of Chemical Research","vol":"44","pages":"101–110","doi":"10.1021/ar1000956","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["span"]},{"id":"kozuch2011b","authors":"S. Kozuch, J. M. L. Martin","year":2011,"title":"The rate-determining step is dead. Long live the rate-determining state!","journal":"ChemPhysChem","vol":"12","pages":"1413–1418","doi":"10.1002/cphc.201100137","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["span"]},{"id":"schreiner2011","authors":"P. R. Schreiner, H. P. Reisenauer, D. Ley, D. Gerbig, C.-H. Wu, W. D. Allen","year":2011,"title":"Methylhydroxycarbene: tunneling control of a chemical reaction","journal":"Science","vol":"332","pages":"1300–1303","doi":"10.1126/science.1203761","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["tunnel"]},{"id":"simmons2012","authors":"E. M. Simmons, J. F. Hartwig","year":2012,"title":"On the interpretation of deuterium kinetic isotope effects in C–H bond functionalizations by transition-metal complexes","journal":"Angewandte Chemie International Edition","vol":"51","pages":"3066–3072","doi":"10.1002/anie.201107334","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["kie"]},{"id":"klinman2013","authors":"J. P. Klinman, A. Kohen","year":2013,"title":"Hydrogen tunneling links protein dynamics to enzyme catalysis","journal":"Annual Review of Biochemistry","vol":"82","pages":"471–496","doi":"10.1146/annurev-biochem-051710-133623","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["kie"]},{"id":"medford2015","authors":"A. J. Medford, A. Vojvodic, J. S. Hummelshøj, J. Voss, F. Abild-Pedersen, F. Studt, T. Bligaard, A. Nilsson, J. K. Nørskov","year":2015,"title":"From the Sabatier principle to a predictive theory of transition-metal heterogeneous catalysis","journal":"Journal of Catalysis","vol":"328","pages":"36–42","doi":"10.1016/j.jcat.2014.12.033","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["micro"]},{"id":"medford2015c","authors":"A. J. Medford, C. Shi, M. J. Hoffmann, A. C. Lausche, S. R. Fitzgibbon, T. Bligaard, J. K. Nørskov","year":2015,"title":"CatMAP: a software package for descriptor-based microkinetic mapping of catalytic trends","journal":"Catalysis Letters","vol":"145","pages":"794–807","doi":"10.1007/s10562-015-1495-6","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["micro"]},{"id":"richardson2018","authors":"J. O. Richardson","year":2018,"title":"Ring-polymer instanton theory","journal":"International Reviews in Physical Chemistry","vol":"37","pages":"171–216","doi":"10.1080/0144235X.2018.1472353","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["tunnel"]},{"id":"grambow2020","authors":"C. A. Grambow, L. Pattanaik, W. H. Green","year":2020,"title":"Reactants, products, and transition states of elementary chemical reactions based on quantum chemistry","journal":"Scientific Data","vol":"7","pages":"137","doi":"10.1038/s41597-020-0460-4","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["paths"]},{"id":"duan2023","authors":"C. Duan, Y. Du, H. Jia, H. J. Kulik","year":2023,"title":"Accurate transition state generation with an object-aware equivariant elementary reaction diffusion model","journal":"Nature Computational Science","vol":"3","pages":"1045–1055","doi":"10.1038/s43588-023-00563-7","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["paths"]}],"chapters":{"pes":{"id":"pes","body":[{"p":"Nuclei are more than three orders of magnitude heavier than electrons, so one can first fix the nuclei and solve the electronic Schrödinger equation; the energy <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math> then depends only on where the nuclei are. This is the Born–Oppenheimer approximation, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math> is the <b>potential energy surface</b>. A non-linear molecule of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></semantics></math> atoms has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></semantics></math> internal coordinates, so the “surface” is really a high-dimensional hypersurface: three dimensions for water, already 21 for ethanol. A chemical reaction is the nuclei travelling across this surface from one valley to another <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-truhlar1996\" data-ref=\"truhlar1996\">Truhlar et al. 1996</a>]</span>."},{"h":"Stationary points and the Hessian"},{"p":"Points where the gradient <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∇</mi><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></semantics></math> are stationary points. What a stationary point is depends on the eigenvalues of the matrix of second derivatives (the Hessian) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>E</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>: all positive is a <b>minimum</b> (a stable molecule or intermediate); exactly one negative is a <b>first-order saddle point</b>, the <b>transition state</b>; higher-order saddles with two or more negative eigenvalues are not places a reaction passes through. After mass-weighting, the eigenvalues of the Hessian are the squared vibrational frequencies, so a transition state has one and only one <b>imaginary frequency</b>, whose eigenvector shows how the atoms move together as they cross the barrier."},{"math":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∇</mi><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mo>‡</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi>H</mi><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">v</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">v</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mo>⋯</mo><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Definition of a transition state: zero gradient and exactly one negative Hessian eigenvalue, whose vibrational frequency ω₁ = √λ₁ is imaginary."},{"p":"The surface of a real molecule cannot be drawn, so teaching and algorithm testing often use two-dimensional model potentials. The demo below uses the <b>Müller–Brown potential</b>: a sum of four Gaussian terms with three minima and two saddle points, designed in 1979 specifically to test saddle-point searches <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-muller1979\" data-ref=\"muller1979\">Müller &amp; Brown 1979</a>]</span>. The positions and energies of its five stationary points have accepted literature values, and the Newton search in the demo finds them again from wherever you click."},{"demo":"pes"},{"p":"Two things are worth keeping in mind on this map. First, a transition state is not a molecule you can “hold”: it is the lowest point on a <b>dividing surface</b> in phase space that separates reactants from products, and the system lingers there for about one vibrational period (tens of femtoseconds). Second, steepest descent from the saddle along the negative-curvature direction, in both directions, gives the <b>minimum energy path</b> joining reactants and products; its arc length is what is usually called “the reaction coordinate”, and the next chapter is about how to compute it."},{"h":"Barriers and reaction energies: Hammond, BEP, Marcus"},{"p":"Within a family of similar reactions, the more exothermic ones tend to have lower barriers. In the 1930s Bell, and Evans and Polanyi, wrote this as a linear relation <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>‡</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>α</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>E</mi></mrow></semantics></math> (Bell–Evans–Polanyi, BEP) <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-bell1936\" data-ref=\"bell1936\">Bell 1936</a>; <a href=\"#ref-evans1935\" data-ref=\"evans1935\">Evans &amp; Polanyi 1935</a>]</span>; in 1955 Hammond gave the geometric version: the transition state of an exothermic reaction resembles the reactants (“early”), that of an endothermic one the products (“late”) <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-hammond1955\" data-ref=\"hammond1955\">Hammond 1955</a>]</span>. In his theory of electron transfer Marcus drew reactants and products as two parabolas whose crossing is the transition state, giving the quadratic relation <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>‡</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>∘</mo></msup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi></mrow></semantics></math> is the reorganisation energy <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-marcus1956\" data-ref=\"marcus1956\">Marcus 1956</a>]</span>."},{"demo":"hammond"},{"p":"The three are really three readings of the same picture. The parabolas cross at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>‡</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>∘</mo></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>: the more exothermic the reaction, the closer the crossing lies to the reactants, which is Hammond; differentiating with respect to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow></semantics></math> gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>‡</mo></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>‡</mo></msup></mrow></semantics></math>, roughly constant when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow></semantics></math> does not vary much, which is the BEP slope <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi></mrow></semantics></math> (Leffler interpreted it as the position of the transition state along the reaction coordinate). Marcus’s quadratic also predicts something a linear relation cannot: once <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, making the reaction more exothermic <i>raises</i> the barrier, the <b>inverted region</b>. It was confirmed experimentally in the 1980s and is part of the work for which Marcus received the 1992 Nobel Prize in Chemistry <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-marcus1993\" data-ref=\"marcus1993\">Marcus 1993</a>]</span>."},{"p":"Organic chemistry often uses one more decomposition: the barrier is split into the energy needed to deform the reactants into their transition-state shapes (the distortion energy) and the interaction between the deformed fragments (the interaction energy). Ess and Houk used it to explain the reactivity of 1,3-dipolar cycloadditions: the barriers are governed mainly by the distortion energy, not by how exothermic the reaction is <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-ess2007\" data-ref=\"ess2007\">Ess &amp; Houk 2007</a>]</span>. All such models are <b>empirical rules</b>: they work well within a family of similar reactions, and the slope and intercept must be refitted for another family."},{"note":"<b>Elsewhere on the site.</b> The <a href=\"../chemistry/computational/#c-pes\">potential energy surfaces</a> chapter of the Computational Chemistry hall explains how <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math> is computed from quantum chemistry; the <a href=\"../group-theory/#c-vibrations\">molecular vibrations</a> chapter of Group Theory classifies the Hessian’s eigenvectors by symmetry; and <a href=\"../nequip/#c-forces\">NequIP</a> covers machine-learned potentials that replace quantum chemistry for energies and forces.","kind":"info"}],"points":[{"h":"Transition state = first-order saddle","p":"Zero gradient and exactly one negative Hessian eigenvalue: exactly one imaginary frequency in a vibrational analysis.","st":"proved"},{"h":"The surface is a product of the Born–Oppenheimer approximation","p":"It breaks down near crossings and conical intersections of electronic states, which is often where photochemistry happens.","st":"approx"},{"h":"Müller–Brown has accepted answers","p":"Three minima and two saddles; the lowest saddle lies at (−0.822, 0.624) with energy −40.66.","st":"history"},{"h":"Hammond: exothermic early, endothermic late","p":"In the two-parabola model x‡ = ½ + ΔG°/(2λ) is an exact geometric result.","st":"proved"},{"h":"BEP is a linear approximation within one family","p":"The slope α lies between 0 and 1; comparisons across families are unreliable.","st":"approx"},{"h":"The Marcus inverted region","p":"When −ΔG° &gt; λ the barrier rises with exothermicity; confirmed experimentally in the 1980s.","st":"tested"}],"math":[{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>E</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msubsup><mi>ω</mi><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mtext>eig</mtext><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mi>H</mi><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The Hessian and the mass-weighted vibrational frequencies; a transition state has one ω² &lt; 0."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>‡</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>α</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>E</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The Bell–Evans–Polanyi relation <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-bell1936\" data-ref=\"bell1936\">Bell 1936</a>; <a href=\"#ref-evans1935\" data-ref=\"evans1935\">Evans &amp; Polanyi 1935</a>]</span>."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>‡</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>∘</mo></msup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Marcus’s quadratic relation; λ is the reorganisation energy <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-marcus1956\" data-ref=\"marcus1956\">Marcus 1956</a>]</span>."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>‡</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>‡</mo></msup></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The crossing position equals the local BEP slope (Leffler’s interpretation)."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>4</mn></munderover><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mtext> ⁣</mtext><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">[</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">]</mo></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The Müller–Brown model potential <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-muller1979\" data-ref=\"muller1979\">Müller &amp; Brown 1979</a>]</span>."}],"example":{"q":"A family of electron-transfer reactions has reorganisation energy <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow></semantics></math> kJ/mol. What are the barriers for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow></semantics></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow></semantics></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>150</mn></mrow></semantics></math> kJ/mol? Where is the transition state in the first case?","steps":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow></semantics></math>: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>‡</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>400</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow></semantics></math> kJ/mol; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>‡</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>20</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>200</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0.40</mn></mrow></semantics></math>, on the reactant side (Hammond).","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>100</mn><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></semantics></math>: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>‡</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></semantics></math>, barrierless, the fastest rate.","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>150</mn></mrow></semantics></math>: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>‡</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>50</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>400</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>6.25</mn></mrow></semantics></math> kJ/mol, higher than at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow></semantics></math>: the inverted region."],"a":"16, 0 and 6.25 kJ/mol; in the first case x‡ = 0.40, which is also the BEP slope. Set λ = 100 in the “barriers and reaction energies” demo to see all three points."},"cites":"<a href=\"#ref-evans1935\" data-ref=\"evans1935\">Evans &amp; Polanyi 1935</a> · <a href=\"#ref-bell1936\" data-ref=\"bell1936\">Bell 1936</a> · <a href=\"#ref-hammond1955\" data-ref=\"hammond1955\">Hammond 1955</a> · <a href=\"#ref-marcus1956\" data-ref=\"marcus1956\">Marcus 1956</a> · <a href=\"#ref-muller1979\" data-ref=\"muller1979\">Müller &amp; Brown 1979</a> · <a href=\"#ref-marcus1993\" data-ref=\"marcus1993\">Marcus 1993</a> · <a href=\"#ref-truhlar1996\" data-ref=\"truhlar1996\">Truhlar et al. 1996</a> · <a href=\"#ref-ess2007\" data-ref=\"ess2007\">Ess &amp; Houk 2007</a>"},"paths":{"id":"paths","body":[{"p":"Optimisers are best at finding minima: follow the negative gradient and the energy must fall. Saddles are hard because they are minima in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></semantics></math> directions and a maximum in just one, and we do not know in advance which. Plain Newton steps (using the Hessian each time, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∇</mi><mi>E</mi></mrow></semantics></math>) converge to a saddle if the start is close enough, but from a little further away they fall into another stationary point; click around in the first chapter’s demo and you will see that where Newton lands depends on where it starts. In practice two kinds of method are used: one knows the reactants and products and looks for the path joining them (chain-of-states methods); the other knows only one starting point and climbs along the softest direction (single-ended methods)."},{"h":"The nudged elastic band (NEB)"},{"p":"NEB places a chain of structures (“images”) between reactants and products, joins neighbours with springs and optimises them all at once. The key is the “nudging”: on each image only the component of the true force <b>perpendicular to the path</b> is kept, so the chain drops into the valley, and only the component of the spring force <b>along the path</b> is kept, so the images spread evenly instead of sliding into the end minima. The chain then settles onto the <b>minimum energy path</b>, and the spring constant barely affects its shape <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-mills1994\" data-ref=\"mills1994\">Mills &amp; Jónsson 1994</a>]</span>. Henkelman and Jónsson later improved how the tangent is chosen (picking a side by the energies of the neighbouring images), which stops the path from kinking at bends <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-henkelman2000t\" data-ref=\"henkelman2000t\">Henkelman &amp; Jónsson 2000</a>]</span>."},{"math":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∇</mi><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">∣</mo><mo>⊥</mo></msub><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">F</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msubsup><msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">∣</mo><mo>∥</mo></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">F</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msubsup><msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">∣</mo><mo>∥</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">R</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">R</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><msub><mover accent=\"true\"><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">τ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The force on image i in NEB: the perpendicular part of the true force plus the parallel part of the spring force. τ̂ is the unit tangent of the path."},{"p":"In a plain NEB the images are evenly spaced, so the saddle usually falls between two of them and the highest image only approximates it. The <b>climbing image</b> fix is simple: once the chain is roughly converged, remove the springs from the highest-energy image and reverse the component of its true force along the tangent. That image then climbs up along the path while relaxing downhill perpendicular to it, and comes to rest exactly on the saddle, at no extra cost in images <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-henkelman2000\" data-ref=\"henkelman2000\">Henkelman et al. 2000</a>]</span>. The demo below runs NEB on the Müller–Brown potential and then switches on the climbing image, so you can watch the highest image climb onto saddle S1."},{"demo":"neb"},{"p":"A close relative of NEB is the <b>string method</b>: the path is treated as a continuous curve; each step first lets the points fall perpendicular to it and then re-interpolates them evenly along the curve, with no springs <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-e2002\" data-ref=\"e2002\">E et al. 2002</a>]</span>. Converged, both give the same minimum energy path. In practice NEB and CI-NEB are the workhorses of surface-catalysis and solid-state diffusion studies (VASP, ASE, Quantum ESPRESSO and others have ready implementations), while molecular work more often uses Hessian-based saddle optimisation."},{"h":"Single-ended methods: the dimer method"},{"p":"If the product is unknown, one can only start from a minimum and climb. The <b>dimer method</b> estimates the local curvature with two closely spaced structures (a “dimer”): rotating the dimer to minimise the sum of the two end energies aligns it with the lowest-curvature (most negative) direction; the force along that direction is then reversed while all other directions descend as usual. Only gradients are needed, never a Hessian, so it works for systems of thousands of atoms and is often combined with kinetic Monte Carlo to discover possible processes in solids automatically <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-henkelman1999\" data-ref=\"henkelman1999\">Henkelman &amp; Jónsson 1999</a>]</span>."},{"h":"IRC: walking down from the saddle to see what it connects"},{"p":"Finding a first-order saddle is not the same as finding the transition state you want: it may connect a different pair of minima. Fukui defined the <b>intrinsic reaction coordinate</b> (IRC): in mass-weighted coordinates, push a small step from the saddle along the imaginary-mode eigenvector in each direction, then follow steepest descent all the way to the minima <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-fukui1970\" data-ref=\"fukui1970\">Fukui 1970</a>]</span>. The Gonzalez–Schlegel algorithm takes each step as a constrained optimisation on a hypersphere, allowing larger steps without leaving the path; it is the most widely used IRC algorithm in quantum-chemistry programs today <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-gonzalez1989\" data-ref=\"gonzalez1989\">Gonzalez &amp; Schlegel 1989</a>]</span>."},{"demo":"irc"},{"p":"The IRC from S1 ends in the global minimum MA on one side and in the intermediate minimum MC, not MB, on the other: on this map, getting from MA to MB means passing two transition states and one intermediate. Real reactions are the same: one “reaction” is often several elementary steps in a row, and each needs its own saddle search and its own IRC."},{"p":"To try this on a real molecule: <a href=\"../models/?job=ts#xtb\">“Transition state and IRC” on the model interface</a> hands a guess of the transition state (or a reactant and a product, which go through NEB and a climbing image first) to Sella on the owner's computer; it finds the first-order saddle with GFN2-xTB, MACE or UMA, computes the imaginary frequency from a finite-difference Hessian, runs the IRC in both directions, draws the energy profile and can play the reaction path and the imaginary mode in 3D. It opens with the HCN ⇌ HNC isomerisation filled in."},{"note":"<b>Machine learning is changing this step.</b> Transition-state searching is among the most labour-intensive parts of computational chemistry. Large datasets now exist, such as the reactants, products and transition states of about twelve thousand gas-phase elementary reactions computed with DFT by Grambow and co-workers <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-grambow2020\" data-ref=\"grambow2020\">Grambow et al. 2020</a>]</span>, and there are models that generate transition-state structures directly, such as the equivariant diffusion model OA-ReactDiff of Duan and co-workers, which proposes a transition state in one shot given reactants and products <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-duan2023\" data-ref=\"duan2023\">Duan et al. 2023</a>]</span>. Their structures still have to be checked with quantum chemistry (imaginary frequency, IRC); the most reliable use today is as good starting points for the traditional methods.","kind":"info"}],"points":[{"h":"NEB: perpendicular true force + parallel spring force","p":"The converged chain lies on the minimum energy path, nearly independent of the spring constant.","st":"tested"},{"h":"The climbing image lands exactly on the saddle","p":"Remove its springs and reverse the tangential force; in the demo the highest image converges to the coordinates and energy of S1.","st":"tested"},{"h":"The dimer method needs only gradients","p":"A pair of structures estimates the lowest-curvature direction; suited to large systems, but which saddle it finds depends on the start.","st":"tested"},{"h":"The IRC is a definition, not a dynamical trajectory","p":"A zero-temperature, infinitely slow steepest-descent path; real molecules carry kinetic energy and deviate from it.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Confirm every saddle with an IRC","p":"Even with a plausible imaginary mode, it may connect other minima.","st":"tested"},{"h":"Generative models provide starting points","p":"Diffusion models and large datasets speed up transition-state searches, but quantum-chemical checks are still needed.","st":"frontier"}],"math":[{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∇</mi><mi>E</mi></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The Newton step: fast near a saddle, but wanders to other stationary points from far away."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">F</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∇</mi><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">R</mi><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mtext> </mtext><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∇</mi><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">R</mi><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>⋅</mo><mover accent=\"true\"><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">τ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><mover accent=\"true\"><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">τ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The force on the climbing image: tangential part reversed, no springs <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-henkelman2000\" data-ref=\"henkelman2000\">Henkelman et al. 2000</a>]</span>."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi>s</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∇</mi><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∇</mi><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">x</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"IRC: steepest descent in mass-weighted coordinates, s the arc length <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-fukui1970\" data-ref=\"fukui1970\">Fukui 1970</a>]</span>."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mover accent=\"true\"><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">N</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≈</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>⋅</mo><mover accent=\"true\"><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">N</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>R</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The dimer method estimates the curvature along N̂ from the forces at its two ends <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-henkelman1999\" data-ref=\"henkelman1999\">Henkelman &amp; Jónsson 1999</a>]</span>."}],"example":{"q":"A CI-NEB for a surface reaction uses 7 intermediate images and needs 150 steps to converge. Each image needs one DFT force calculation per step, taking 4 minutes. How long does it take serially, and with the 7 images computed in parallel? What if the dimer method is used instead, with 2 force calls per step and 300 steps?","steps":["Serial: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>150</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>4200</mn></mrow></semantics></math> minutes = 70 hours.","7 images in parallel: each step waits only 4 minutes, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>150</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>600</mn></mrow></semantics></math> minutes = 10 hours.","Dimer: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>300</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>2400</mn></mrow></semantics></math> minutes = 40 hours (20 if the two force calls run in parallel), and the saddle it finds need not be the one leading to your product."],"a":"70 hours serial, 10 hours parallel; 40 hours for the dimer method. Each saddle then still needs a frequency calculation and an IRC."},"cites":"<a href=\"#ref-fukui1970\" data-ref=\"fukui1970\">Fukui 1970</a> · <a href=\"#ref-gonzalez1989\" data-ref=\"gonzalez1989\">Gonzalez &amp; Schlegel 1989</a> · <a href=\"#ref-mills1994\" data-ref=\"mills1994\">Mills &amp; Jónsson 1994</a> · <a href=\"#ref-henkelman1999\" data-ref=\"henkelman1999\">Henkelman &amp; Jónsson 1999</a> · <a href=\"#ref-henkelman2000\" data-ref=\"henkelman2000\">Henkelman et al. 2000</a> · <a href=\"#ref-henkelman2000t\" data-ref=\"henkelman2000t\">Henkelman &amp; Jónsson 2000</a> · <a href=\"#ref-e2002\" data-ref=\"e2002\">E et al. 2002</a> · <a href=\"#ref-grambow2020\" data-ref=\"grambow2020\">Grambow et al. 2020</a> · <a href=\"#ref-duan2023\" data-ref=\"duan2023\">Duan et al. 2023</a>"},"tst":{"id":"tst","body":[{"p":"In 1889 Arrhenius summarised a large body of measurements as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>A</mi><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math>, interpreting <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> as the “activation” energy molecules must first acquire <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-arrhenius1889\" data-ref=\"arrhenius1889\">Arrhenius 1889</a>]</span>. The formula works well, but <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></semantics></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> are just fitted parameters. More than forty years later, with quantum and statistical mechanics in hand, Eyring and Evans and Polanyi gave, almost simultaneously in 1935, a way to compute rate constants from the potential energy surface: <b>transition state theory</b> (TST), also called activated complex theory <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-eyring1935\" data-ref=\"eyring1935\">Eyring 1935</a>; <a href=\"#ref-evans1935\" data-ref=\"evans1935\">Evans &amp; Polanyi 1935</a>]</span>. Laidler and King’s review tells the history well <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-laidler1983\" data-ref=\"laidler1983\">Laidler &amp; King 1983</a>]</span>."},{"h":"The skeleton of the derivation"},{"p":"Take a dividing surface through the saddle, perpendicular to the reaction coordinate. Assume (1) the reactants stay in thermal equilibrium with the systems crossing the surface; (2) systems that cross never come back; and (3) motion along the reaction coordinate separates from the other degrees of freedom and is classical. The rate is then the equilibrium <b>flux</b> through the surface. Integrating out the reaction-coordinate degree of freedom contributes exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mi>T</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>h</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, and the rest is written as the partition function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mo>‡</mo></msup></mrow></semantics></math> of the transition state <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-wigner1938\" data-ref=\"wigner1938\">Wigner 1938</a>]</span>:"},{"math":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mi>T</mi></mrow><mi>h</mi></mfrac><mtext> </mtext><mfrac><msup><mi>Q</mi><mo>‡</mo></msup><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi></msub></mfrac><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mi>T</mi></mrow><mi>h</mi></mfrac><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>‡</mo></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mi>T</mi></mrow><mi>h</mi></mfrac><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>‡</mo></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>R</mi></mrow></msup><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>‡</mo></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Three forms of the Eyring equation. E₀ is the zero-point-corrected barrier; ΔG‡, ΔH‡ and ΔS‡ are the free energy, enthalpy and entropy of activation (standard state 1 mol/L or 1 bar, which must be stated for bimolecular reactions)."},{"p":"At 25 °C <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mi>T</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6.21</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>12</mn></msup><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math>, roughly the frequency of a molecular vibration. That gives some numbers worth remembering: with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>‡</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>73</mn></mrow></semantics></math> kJ/mol (17.5 kcal/mol) a first-order rate constant is about one per second; every additional <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>5.71</mn></mrow></semantics></math> kJ/mol (1.36 kcal/mol) makes it ten times slower; and a one-hour half-life at room temperature corresponds to 94 kJ/mol (22.5 kcal/mol). In the same year Wynne-Jones and Eyring extended this formulation to reactions in solution, introducing the enthalpy and entropy of activation <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-wynnejones1935\" data-ref=\"wynnejones1935\">Wynne-Jones &amp; Eyring 1935</a>]</span>."},{"demo":"eyring"},{"h":"Eyring plots and Arrhenius plots"},{"p":"Plotting <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math> against <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>T</mi></mrow></semantics></math> gives slope <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>‡</mo></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>R</mi></mrow></semantics></math> and intercept <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>‡</mo></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>R</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, the standard way to get activation parameters from variable-temperature kinetics. For unimolecular (or solution) reactions the two sets of parameters convert simply: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>‡</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi></mrow></semantics></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>e</mi><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mi>T</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>‡</mo></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>R</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math>; for gas-phase bimolecular reactions <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>‡</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi></mrow></semantics></math>. But beware: the intercept is an extrapolation from the experimental temperatures to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></semantics></math>, tens of times further than the temperature range itself, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>‡</mo></msup></mrow></semantics></math> carries large errors that are strongly correlated with those of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>‡</mo></msup></mrow></semantics></math>. Much of what is called “enthalpy–entropy compensation” is an artefact of exactly this statistical correlation."},{"demo":"plot"},{"h":"Where the assumptions fail: κ"},{"p":"The no-recrossing assumption makes TST an <b>upper bound</b> on the rate: any trajectory that turns back can only lower the true rate. This leads to <b>variational transition state theory</b> (VTST): move the dividing surface along the reaction coordinate and take the position of minimum flux, often more accurate than placing it at the saddle and essential for reactions without a clear barrier <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-truhlar1984\" data-ref=\"truhlar1984\">Truhlar &amp; Garrett 1984</a>; <a href=\"#ref-truhlar1996\" data-ref=\"truhlar1996\">Truhlar et al. 1996</a>]</span>. The remaining deviations are collected in the transmission coefficient <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>κ</mi><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math>."},{"p":"In solution, the solvent drags on motion along the reaction coordinate like friction, so systems diffuse back and forth near the barrier top and recross many times. In 1940 Kramers wrote this as a Langevin equation and found, at moderate to high friction, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt><mo>−</mo><mi>γ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math> (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> the curvature frequency of the barrier top, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi></mrow></semantics></math> the friction); at very low friction the trend reverses because energy exchange is too slow, and the rate grows with friction, giving a “Kramers turnover” in between <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-kramers1940\" data-ref=\"kramers1940\">Kramers 1940</a>; <a href=\"#ref-hanggi1990\" data-ref=\"hanggi1990\">Hänggi et al. 1990</a>]</span>. On the other hand, light atoms <b>tunnel</b> through the barrier, making <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></semantics></math>; chapter 5 is devoted to that. Pollak and Talkner’s review summarises where these lines of work stand today <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-pollak2005\" data-ref=\"pollak2005\">Pollak &amp; Talkner 2005</a>]</span>."},{"note":"<b>Where a computed ΔG‡ comes from.</b> A quantum-chemistry program gives the electronic energies and vibrational frequencies of reactant and transition state; ideal-gas, rigid-rotor and harmonic-oscillator partition functions then give <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>‡</mo></msup></mrow></semantics></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>‡</mo></msup></mrow></semantics></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>‡</mo></msup></mrow></semantics></math>. Low-frequency vibrations (&lt; 100 cm⁻¹) inflate the entropy in the harmonic model and are usually treated with quasi-harmonic corrections; solution reactions also need solvation free energies and the change of standard state from 1 bar to 1 mol/L (7.9 kJ/mol per molecule at 25 °C). All of these are approximations, and DFT barriers themselves often carry errors of order 10 kJ/mol, so computed rate constants being off by one or two orders of magnitude is common. The <a href=\"../code/\">Code Desk</a> writes transition-state optimisation and frequency inputs for ORCA, Gaussian, xTB and other programs.","kind":"warn"}],"points":[{"h":"k_BT/h = 6.21 × 10¹² s⁻¹ (25 °C)","p":"The contribution of the reaction-coordinate degree of freedom; independent of the reaction.","st":"proved"},{"h":"5.71 kJ/mol = a factor of ten","p":"RT ln 10 at 298 K; 73 kJ/mol is about once a second, 94 kJ/mol a half-life of about an hour.","st":"proved"},{"h":"TST is an upper bound on the rate","p":"In classical mechanics recrossing can only lower the rate; VTST picks the dividing surface with minimum flux.","st":"proved"},{"h":"ΔS‡ comes from a long extrapolation","p":"Its error is strongly correlated with that of ΔH‡; “enthalpy–entropy compensation” is often a statistical artefact.","st":"tested"},{"h":"Ea = ΔH‡ + RT","p":"For unimolecular or solution reactions; + 2RT for gas-phase bimolecular reactions.","st":"proved"},{"h":"κ collects every deviation","p":"Recrossing and solvent friction make κ &lt; 1 (Kramers); tunnelling makes κ &gt; 1.","st":"approx"}],"math":[{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>A</mi><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The Arrhenius equation <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-arrhenius1889\" data-ref=\"arrhenius1889\">Arrhenius 1889</a>]</span>."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>κ</mi><mtext> </mtext><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mi>T</mi></mrow><mi>h</mi></mfrac><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>‡</mo></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The Eyring equation with a transmission coefficient <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-eyring1935\" data-ref=\"eyring1935\">Eyring 1935</a>]</span>; Evans and Polanyi reached it independently the same year <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-evans1935\" data-ref=\"evans1935\">Evans &amp; Polanyi 1935</a>]</span>."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mfrac><mi>k</mi><mi>T</mi></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mi>h</mi></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>‡</mo></msup></mrow><mi>R</mi></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>‡</mo></msup></mrow><mi>R</mi></mfrac><mtext> </mtext><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>T</mi></mfrac></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The Eyring plot: the slope gives ΔH‡, the intercept ΔS‡."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>‡</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>e</mi><mtext> </mtext><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mi>T</mi></mrow><mi>h</mi></mfrac><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>‡</mo></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>R</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Converting between the two sets of activation parameters (unimolecular)."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">(</mo><mfrac><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Kramers’s moderate-to-high-friction result <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-kramers1940\" data-ref=\"kramers1940\">Kramers 1940</a>]</span>; see <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-hanggi1990\" data-ref=\"hanggi1990\">Hänggi et al. 1990</a>]</span> for the review fifty years on."}],"example":{"q":"A first-order reaction has rate constant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.0</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math> at 25.0 °C and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.6</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math> at 45.0 °C. Find <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>‡</mo></msup></mrow></semantics></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>‡</mo></msup></mrow></semantics></math> (25 °C), <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>‡</mo></msup></mrow></semantics></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>.","steps":["Two-point Eyring plot: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>‡</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>R</mi><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mtext> ⁣</mtext><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">[</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">]</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">/</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>79.4</mn></mrow></semantics></math> kJ/mol.","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>‡</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mi>T</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>h</mi></mrow><mi>k</mi></mfrac><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>2.479</mn><mo>×</mo><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>6.21</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>12</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2.0</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>94.1</mn></mrow></semantics></math> kJ/mol.","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>‡</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>‡</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>‡</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>79.4</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>94.1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>×</mo><mn>1000</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>298.15</mn><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>49</mn></mrow></semantics></math> J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹: the transition state is more ordered than the reactant.","Two-point Arrhenius: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>R</mi><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>82.0</mn></mrow></semantics></math> kJ/mol, exceeding <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>‡</mo></msup></mrow></semantics></math> by just <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>T</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover><mo>≈</mo><mn>2.6</mn></mrow></semantics></math> kJ/mol."],"a":"ΔH‡ ≈ 79 kJ/mol, ΔG‡ ≈ 94 kJ/mol, ΔS‡ ≈ −49 J/(mol K), Ea ≈ 82 kJ/mol. With only two temperatures ΔS‡ is very uncertain; increase the noise in the “Eyring plot” demo to see how much."},"cites":"<a href=\"#ref-arrhenius1889\" data-ref=\"arrhenius1889\">Arrhenius 1889</a> · <a href=\"#ref-evans1935\" data-ref=\"evans1935\">Evans &amp; Polanyi 1935</a> · <a href=\"#ref-eyring1935\" data-ref=\"eyring1935\">Eyring 1935</a> · <a href=\"#ref-wynnejones1935\" data-ref=\"wynnejones1935\">Wynne-Jones &amp; Eyring 1935</a> · <a href=\"#ref-wigner1938\" data-ref=\"wigner1938\">Wigner 1938</a> · <a href=\"#ref-kramers1940\" data-ref=\"kramers1940\">Kramers 1940</a> · <a href=\"#ref-laidler1983\" data-ref=\"laidler1983\">Laidler &amp; King 1983</a> · <a href=\"#ref-truhlar1984\" data-ref=\"truhlar1984\">Truhlar &amp; Garrett 1984</a> · <a href=\"#ref-hanggi1990\" data-ref=\"hanggi1990\">Hänggi et al. 1990</a> · <a href=\"#ref-truhlar1996\" data-ref=\"truhlar1996\">Truhlar et al. 1996</a> · <a href=\"#ref-pollak2005\" data-ref=\"pollak2005\">Pollak &amp; Talkner 2005</a>"},"rrkm":{"id":"rrkm","body":[{"p":"Reactions such as the isomerisation of cyclopropane to propene or the decomposition of N₂O₅ have a single reactant, yet their rates depend on pressure: first order at high pressure, second order at low pressure (proportional also to the concentration of other gases). Lindemann explained this in 1922: a molecule is first “activated” to A* by collision with another molecule M, after which A* is either “deactivated” by another collision or reacts by itself <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-lindemann1922\" data-ref=\"lindemann1922\">Lindemann 1922</a>]</span>:"},{"math":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi></mrow><mi><munder><mo><mo><mover><mo><mo>⇌</mo></mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mover></mo></mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></munder></mi><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi></mrow><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"/><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mover><mo stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"3.0em\">→</mo><mpadded width=\"+0.6em\" lspace=\"0.3em\"><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mpadded></mover><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mspace width=\"1em\"/><mo>⇒</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"/><msub><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></msub><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The Lindemann mechanism and the fall-off curve it predicts: as [M] → ∞, k → k∞ = k₁k₂/k₋₁ (first order); as [M] → 0, k → k₀[M] = k₁[M] (second order)."},{"p":"Lindemann’s expression is qualitatively right but quantitatively far off: measured fall-off curves are much broader, and the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math> it implies often exceeds the collision frequency. The problem is that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math> is not a constant: more energetic A* react faster. Hinshelwood first pointed out that the energy in all of a molecule’s vibrations must be counted; in the late 1920s Rice, Ramsperger and Kassel (RRK) assumed the energy is distributed randomly among <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></semantics></math> vibrational modes and obtained <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>ν</mi><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>E</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math>: with enough energy there will always be a moment when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math> happens to gather in the bond that breaks."},{"h":"RRKM: counting states"},{"p":"In 1952 Marcus combined RRK with transition state theory: apply a “microcanonical” transition state theory to an ensemble of molecules all with energy <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, so the rate is the flux through the dividing surface divided by the number of reactant states <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-marcus1952\" data-ref=\"marcus1952\">Marcus 1952</a>]</span>. The result is remarkably simple:"},{"math":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>‡</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The RRKM formula. N‡ is the total number of states of the transition state (lacking the reaction-coordinate mode) below energy E − E₀, ρ(E) the reactant density of states at E, and σ the reaction-path degeneracy."},{"p":"The key assumption is that <b>intramolecular vibrational energy redistribution</b> (IVR) is much faster than reaction, so every state at energy <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi></mrow></semantics></math> is visited equally often, the same thing as the ergodic hypothesis of statistical mechanics. For most medium-sized molecules under thermal conditions this is a good approximation; it fails for ultrafast pulsed excitation, very small molecules or special “bottlenecks”, which then show “non-RRKM” behaviour."},{"p":"What remains is counting states. For harmonic oscillators the algorithm published by Beyer and Swinehart in 1973 needs one array and two loops: set bin 0 to 1, then for each vibrational frequency <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, run <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>+</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></semantics></math> starting from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi></mrow></semantics></math> <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-beyer1973\" data-ref=\"beyer1973\">Beyer &amp; Swinehart 1973</a>]</span>. Afterwards <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></semantics></math> is the number of states whose energy falls in bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, and the cumulative sum is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>. Stein and Rabinovitch extended it to anharmonic oscillators and hindered rotors <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-stein1973\" data-ref=\"stein1973\">Stein &amp; Rabinovitch 1973</a>]</span>. The algorithm is really expanding the generating function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∏</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math>, so it is exact up to the bin size."},{"demo":"rrkm"},{"p":"The demo shows two easily overlooked facts. First, classical RRK is much smaller than quantum RRKM at moderate energies: the classical density of states gets the discreteness of states near the zero-point level wrong. Second, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math> jumps from zero at the threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math> and then rises extremely fast, spanning a dozen orders of magnitude, so “the lifetime of A*” is not one number but a very broad distribution."},{"h":"From k(E) to the thermal rate constant: fall-off"},{"p":"The thermal rate constant averages <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math> over the energy distribution. The simplest “strong-collision” assumption is that every collision throws the molecule straight back into the Boltzmann distribution, giving <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>E</mi></msub><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></semantics></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi></mrow></semantics></math> the collision frequency. In the high-pressure limit this is exactly the harmonic transition state theory of chapter 3; in the low-pressure limit it is the collision frequency times the fraction of molecules above <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math>. Real collisions transfer only a few hundred to a few thousand cm⁻¹ at a time (“weak collisions”) and need a master equation; Troe and co-workers gave practical formulas that write the fall-off curve in a few parameters, the form most used in combustion and atmospheric models <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-gilbert1983\" data-ref=\"gilbert1983\">Gilbert et al. 1983</a>]</span>."},{"demo":"falloff"},{"note":"<b>Current practice.</b> Rate constants for combustion and atmospheric chemistry are increasingly computed directly by “ab initio + RRKM master equation”: high-level quantum chemistry gives the stationary points, variational RRKM treats barrierless dissociations, a master equation handles pressure, and tunnelling corrections are added. The review by Fernández-Ramos and co-workers covers the same set of methods on the bimolecular side <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-fernandez2006\" data-ref=\"fernandez2006\">Fernández-Ramos et al. 2006</a>]</span>. As in chapter 3, the largest single source of error is still the barrier height itself.","kind":"info"}],"points":[{"h":"Lindemann: pressure sets the order","p":"First order at high pressure, second at low; qualitatively right but the fall-off is too narrow.","st":"tested"},{"h":"RRKM: k(E) = σN‡(E − E₀)/(hρ(E))","p":"Microcanonical transition state theory, assuming fast intramolecular energy redistribution.","st":"approx"},{"h":"Beyer–Swinehart counts exactly","p":"It expands the generating function Π(1 − x^ν)⁻¹; the only error is binning. The tests match brute-force enumeration.","st":"proved"},{"h":"High-pressure limit = canonical TST","p":"Averaging k(E) over the Boltzmann distribution gives harmonic transition state theory exactly.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Classical RRK underestimates k(E)","p":"The classical density of states is wrong near the zero-point level; use quantum counting or the Whitten–Rabinovitch correction.","st":"tested"},{"h":"Real collisions are weak","p":"Each transfers only part of the energy; use a master equation or the Troe formulas.","st":"approx"}],"math":[{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></msub><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The Lindemann–Hinshelwood fall-off curve <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-lindemann1922\" data-ref=\"lindemann1922\">Lindemann 1922</a>]</span>."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>E</mi></mfrac><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">)</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Classical RRK: energy randomly distributed over s oscillators."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>‡</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"RRKM <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-marcus1952\" data-ref=\"marcus1952\">Marcus 1952</a>]</span>."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><munder><mo>∑</mo><mi>n</mi></munder><msup><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msup><mo>=</mo><munderover><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>s</mi></munderover><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The generating function of harmonic states, expanded term by term by Beyer–Swinehart <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-beyer1973\" data-ref=\"beyer1973\">Beyer &amp; Swinehart 1973</a>]</span>."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></msubsup><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>E</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>σ</mi><mtext> </mtext><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mi>T</mi></mrow><mi>h</mi></mfrac><mtext> </mtext><mfrac><msup><mi>Q</mi><mo>‡</mo></msup><mi>Q</mi></mfrac><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Averaging RRKM over the Boltzmann distribution recovers transition state theory."}],"example":{"q":"A “molecule” with only three vibrational modes, at 500, 700 and 1100 cm⁻¹. How many states lie at or below 2000 cm⁻¹ above the zero-point level? How long is the Beyer–Swinehart array with 100 cm⁻¹ bins?","steps":["With the 1100 cm⁻¹ mode unexcited: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>500</mn><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>700</mn><mi>b</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>2000</mn></mrow></semantics></math>. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></semantics></math> gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>…</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></semantics></math> (5), <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></semantics></math> gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>…</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></semantics></math> (3), <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></semantics></math> gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></semantics></math> (2): 10 in all.","With one quantum in the 1100 cm⁻¹ mode: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>500</mn><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>700</mn><mi>b</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>900</mn></mrow></semantics></math> gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>, 3 states. Two quanta already exceed 2000.","13 in all. The array covers 0 to 2000 cm⁻¹ in 100 cm⁻¹ bins, 21 bins; three passes of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>+</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></semantics></math> (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi></mrow></semantics></math> = 5, 7 and 11 bins) and a cumulative sum to the last bin give 13."],"a":"13 states. Real molecules have dozens of modes and energies of tens of thousands of cm⁻¹, giving 10¹⁵ states or more, but the algorithm is still these three lines; the tests check it against brute-force enumeration."},"cites":"<a href=\"#ref-lindemann1922\" data-ref=\"lindemann1922\">Lindemann 1922</a> · <a href=\"#ref-marcus1952\" data-ref=\"marcus1952\">Marcus 1952</a> · <a href=\"#ref-beyer1973\" data-ref=\"beyer1973\">Beyer &amp; Swinehart 1973</a> · <a href=\"#ref-stein1973\" data-ref=\"stein1973\">Stein &amp; Rabinovitch 1973</a> · <a href=\"#ref-gilbert1983\" data-ref=\"gilbert1983\">Gilbert et al. 1983</a> · <a href=\"#ref-fernandez2006\" data-ref=\"fernandez2006\">Fernández-Ramos et al. 2006</a>"},"tunnel":{"id":"tunnel","body":[{"p":"Transition state theory treats motion along the reaction coordinate classically: anything below the barrier bounces back, anything above passes. In quantum mechanics both are wrong: below the barrier there is some probability of <b>tunnelling</b> through, and above it some probability of being <b>reflected</b>. The tunnelling probability falls exponentially with the particle’s mass and the barrier width, so it is negligible for heavy atoms but can be large for hydrogen; the one-dimensional problems in the <a href=\"../quantum/physics/#c-wave\">Quantum Physics pavilion</a> show the same physics."},{"p":"The measure of tunnelling is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>‡</mo></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>h</mi><mi>c</mi><msup><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>ν</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>‡</mo></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mi>T</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>ν</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>‡</mo></msup></mrow></semantics></math> the magnitude of the transition state’s imaginary frequency. Hydrogen-transfer reactions often have imaginary frequencies of 1000–2000i cm⁻¹, so at room temperature <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></semantics></math> lies between 5 and 10, not a small number. The thermally averaged tunnelling factor is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mtext>quantum</mtext></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mtext>classical</mtext></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, multiplied into the Eyring equation."},{"h":"Three classic corrections"},{"p":"<b>Wigner</b> (1932): expand the quantum-statistical correction to the classical partition function to order <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> and get <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>24</mn></mrow></semantics></math> <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-wigner1932\" data-ref=\"wigner1932\">Wigner 1932</a>]</span>. It uses only the curvature at the barrier top, so it is reliable only for small <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></semantics></math> (high temperature, broad barriers). <b>Bell</b> (1959): integrate the transmission probability exactly for an infinite parabolic barrier to get <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>, which diverges as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>→</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow></semantics></math> because an inverted parabola gets ever narrower further down and penetration has no lower limit <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-bell1959\" data-ref=\"bell1959\">Bell 1959</a>]</span>; practical calculations truncate the parabola, and Skodje and Truhlar gave an analytic truncated form <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-skodje1981\" data-ref=\"skodje1981\">Skodje &amp; Truhlar 1981</a>]</span>. <b>Eckart</b> (1930): a barrier of finite height on both sides, shaped like a step plus a hump, for which the Schrödinger equation has an exact solution and the transmission probability is a few hyperbolic cosines <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-eckart1930\" data-ref=\"eckart1930\">Eckart 1930</a>]</span>."},{"math":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>β</mi><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>β</mi><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msup><mtext> ⁣</mtext><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></msubsup><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi><mi>E</mi></mrow></msup><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi>E</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mn>24</mn></mfrac><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msub><mi>κ</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The thermally averaged tunnelling factor (P(E) the transmission probability, V₁ the barrier measured from the reactant side; a classical step P gives κ = 1), and the Wigner and Bell approximations."},{"demo":"eckart"},{"p":"The demo plots Eckart’s analytic transmission probability together with a direct numerical solution of the Schrödinger equation (the tests find them equal to seven significant figures). Some regularities: for small <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></semantics></math> all three corrections are close to 1 and to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>24</mn></mrow></semantics></math>; as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></semantics></math> nears <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow></semantics></math> Bell diverges while Eckart stays finite; the lower the barrier (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math> smaller), the less tunnelling matters at the same imaginary frequency, because the window of energies that can get through is narrower. Below the <b>crossover temperature</b> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>‡</mo></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math> tunnelling happens mostly far below the barrier top, and any correction built on the top’s curvature becomes unreliable."},{"h":"Multidimensional tunnelling and instantons"},{"p":"Real tunnelling paths need not follow the minimum energy path: cutting through a higher but narrower stretch of barrier is often more favourable (“corner cutting”). Small-curvature (SCT) and large-curvature tunnelling (LCT) are the standard ways to handle this within variational transition state theory <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-truhlar1984\" data-ref=\"truhlar1984\">Truhlar &amp; Garrett 1984</a>; <a href=\"#ref-truhlar1996\" data-ref=\"truhlar1996\">Truhlar et al. 1996</a>]</span>. Another route is semiclassical <b>instanton</b> theory: find a periodic orbit in imaginary time, which gives the dominant contribution to the tunnelling rate at low temperature <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-miller1975\" data-ref=\"miller1975\">Miller 1975</a>]</span>. Present-day ring-polymer instanton methods combine it with path integrals and apply directly to systems of tens of atoms <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-richardson2018\" data-ref=\"richardson2018\">Richardson 2018</a>]</span>."},{"h":"When tunnelling decides the product"},{"p":"Schreiner, Allen and co-workers prepared methylhydroxycarbene, H₃C–C–OH, in an argon matrix at 11 K. It has two rearrangement routes: to vinyl alcohol over a lower barrier, and to acetaldehyde over a higher but narrower one. It was found to have a half-life of about an hour and to form almost only acetaldehyde, while the deuterated H₃C–C–OD was far more persistent. This was the first clear example of “<b>tunnelling control</b>”: the product is decided neither by the barrier height (kinetic control) nor by product stability (thermodynamic control), but by how narrow the barrier is <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-schreiner2011\" data-ref=\"schreiner2011\">Schreiner et al. 2011</a>]</span>."},{"note":"<b>Tunnelling and isotope effects.</b> Tunnelling probability is extremely sensitive to mass, so its most direct experimental signature is an unusually large kinetic isotope effect with anomalous temperature dependence (curved Arrhenius plots, ratios of pre-exponential factors far from 1). The next chapter covers these.","kind":"info"}],"points":[{"h":"u = hcν‡/k_BT measures how much tunnelling matters","p":"Hydrogen transfers have imaginary frequencies of 1000–2000i cm⁻¹, so u is 5–10 at room temperature.","st":"tested"},{"h":"Wigner is a high-temperature correction","p":"A first-order correction in ħ², using only the barrier-top curvature.","st":"approx"},{"h":"Bell diverges at u = 2π","p":"An infinite parabolic barrier narrows without limit below the top; it must be truncated.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Eckart has an exact solution","p":"Its transmission probability is analytic; the demo agrees with a numerical solution of the Schrödinger equation.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Crossover temperature T_c = ħω‡/(2πk_B)","p":"Below it tunnelling dominates and multidimensional or instanton methods are needed.","st":"approx"},{"h":"Tunnelling control","p":"At 11 K methylhydroxycarbene passes a higher but narrower barrier to give acetaldehyde.","st":"tested"}],"math":[{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>‡</mo></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mi>T</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>h</mi><mi>c</mi><mtext> </mtext><msup><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>ν</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>‡</mo></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mi>T</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1.4388</mn><mtext> </mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi></mrow><mo>×</mo><msup><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>ν</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>‡</mo></msup></mrow><mi>T</mi></mfrac></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The tunnelling parameter; 1.4388 cm K is hc/k_B."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>24</mn></mfrac><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The Wigner correction <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-wigner1932\" data-ref=\"wigner1932\">Wigner 1932</a>]</span>."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi>u</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The infinite parabolic barrier <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-bell1959\" data-ref=\"bell1959\">Bell 1959</a>]</span>."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>cosh</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>cosh</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>cosh</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>cosh</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The transmission probability of the Eckart barrier; a and b are proportional to the wave numbers on either side, d set by the barrier’s height and width <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-eckart1930\" data-ref=\"eckart1930\">Eckart 1930</a>]</span>."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>‡</mo></msup></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The crossover temperature: below it deep tunnelling dominates."}],"example":{"q":"A hydrogen-transfer reaction has an imaginary frequency of 1500i cm⁻¹. What is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></semantics></math> at 300 K? What does the Wigner correction give? Can Bell’s formula be used? What is the crossover temperature?","steps":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.4388</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1500</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>300</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>7.19</mn></mrow></semantics></math>.","Wigner: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>7.1</mn><msup><mn>9</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>24</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>3.16</mn></mrow></semantics></math>, far outside the range where the expansion holds (which needs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>≪</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow></semantics></math>); treat it as an order of magnitude only.","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>7.19</mn><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6.28</mn></mrow></semantics></math>: Bell’s infinite-parabola formula diverges and cannot be used.","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1.4388</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1500</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>343</mn></mrow></semantics></math> K, so 300 K is already below the crossover."],"a":"u ≈ 7.2, Wigner ≈ 3.2 (unreliable), Bell diverges, T_c ≈ 343 K. Eckart or multidimensional tunnelling is needed; set 1500 cm⁻¹ and 300 K in the demo to see what Eckart gives."},"cites":"<a href=\"#ref-eckart1930\" data-ref=\"eckart1930\">Eckart 1930</a> · <a href=\"#ref-wigner1932\" data-ref=\"wigner1932\">Wigner 1932</a> · <a href=\"#ref-bell1959\" data-ref=\"bell1959\">Bell 1959</a> · <a href=\"#ref-miller1975\" data-ref=\"miller1975\">Miller 1975</a> · <a href=\"#ref-skodje1981\" data-ref=\"skodje1981\">Skodje &amp; Truhlar 1981</a> · <a href=\"#ref-truhlar1984\" data-ref=\"truhlar1984\">Truhlar &amp; Garrett 1984</a> · <a href=\"#ref-truhlar1996\" data-ref=\"truhlar1996\">Truhlar et al. 1996</a> · <a href=\"#ref-schreiner2011\" data-ref=\"schreiner2011\">Schreiner et al. 2011</a> · <a href=\"#ref-richardson2018\" data-ref=\"richardson2018\">Richardson 2018</a>"},"kie":{"id":"kie","body":[{"p":"In the Born–Oppenheimer approximation the potential energy surface is determined by the electrons alone and does not depend on nuclear masses. Isotopic substitution therefore leaves the electronic energy of the barrier unchanged and changes the vibrational frequencies: in the diatomic approximation <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>ν</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∝</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msqrt><mi>μ</mi></msqrt></mrow></semantics></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi></mrow></semantics></math> the reduced mass. The C–H stretch is about 2900 cm⁻¹ and C–D about 2130 cm⁻¹, so their zero-point energies <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mi>h</mi><mi>c</mi><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>ν</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></semantics></math> differ by about 385 cm⁻¹ (4.6 kJ/mol)."},{"p":"If that C–H bond breaks in the rate-determining step, then at the transition state the stretch has become the reaction coordinate (the imaginary mode), and its zero-point energy is gone. C–H has more zero-point energy than C–D, so the effective barrier for C–H is 385 cm⁻¹ lower and the rate ratio is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>385</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>207</mn></mrow></msup><mo>≈</mo><mn>6.4</mn></mrow></semantics></math> (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mi>T</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>h</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>207</mn></mrow></semantics></math> cm⁻¹ at 25 °C). That is where the familiar “primary isotope effect of about 7” comes from. Westheimer pointed out in 1961 that when the hydrogen is transferred symmetrically between two heavy atoms at the transition state, the stretch’s zero-point energy is lost most completely and the effect is largest; with an early or late transition state a residual “symmetric stretch” survives in the transition state and the effect is smaller <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-westheimer1961\" data-ref=\"westheimer1961\">Westheimer 1961</a>]</span>."},{"math":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><msub><mi>k</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi></msub><msub><mi>k</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>≈</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mtext> ⁣</mtext><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">[</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>h</mi><mi>c</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mi>T</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msub><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>ν</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msub><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>ν</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi></msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">)</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">]</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>ν</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>ν</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>ν</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">‡</mo></msub></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The semiclassical isotope effect from zero-point energies alone. Δν̃ = ν̃_R − ν̃_‡ is the frequency lost between reactant (R) and transition state (‡); the full theory includes the ratios of all vibrational, rotational and translational partition functions (Bigeleisen–Mayer)."},{"p":"The full theory was set up by Bigeleisen and Mayer in 1947 for isotope-exchange equilibria: reduced partition-function ratios combine the translational and rotational contributions so that the result depends only on vibrational frequencies <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-bigeleisen1947\" data-ref=\"bigeleisen1947\">Bigeleisen &amp; Mayer 1947</a>]</span>. Applying the same formalism to the “equilibrium” between reactant and transition state, and multiplying by the ratio of imaginary frequencies and a tunnelling correction, is the standard way to compute isotope effects today."},{"demo":"kie"},{"h":"Kinds of isotope effects"},{"list":["<b>Primary</b>: the labelled bond breaks or forms in the rate-determining step. Hydrogen primary effects are usually 2–8; heavy-atom effects (¹³C, ¹⁵N, ¹⁸O) are only 1.01–1.10 but can be measured precisely in competition experiments.","<b>Secondary</b>: the labelled bond does not break, but its vibrational environment changes. When carbon goes from sp³ to sp² the out-of-plane C–H bend softens and the α-secondary effect is about 1.1–1.2; the reverse, sp² to sp³, gives values below 1 (inverse isotope effects).","<b>Solvent isotope effects</b>: run the reaction in D₂O to see whether exchangeable protons take part in the rate-determining step.","<b>Equilibrium isotope effects</b>: the same zero-point argument applied to an equilibrium constant; the heavy isotope prefers the side with the stiffer bonds."]},{"h":"Signatures of tunnelling"},{"p":"Tunnelling depends on mass far more strongly than zero-point energy does, so with tunnelling <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> can far exceed 7. When soybean lipoxygenase-1 abstracts a hydrogen from linoleic acid, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> is about 80 at room temperature, nearly independent of temperature and with a very small difference in activation energies, none of which a semiclassical model can explain <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-knapp2002\" data-ref=\"knapp2002\">Knapp et al. 2002</a>]</span>. Klinman and Kohen reviewed the evidence for hydrogen tunnelling in enzymes and its relation to protein dynamics: part of what the enzyme does may be to push donor and acceptor together and so narrow the barrier <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-klinman2013\" data-ref=\"klinman2013\">Klinman &amp; Kohen 2013</a>]</span>."},{"p":"Another criterion is the relation among the three hydrogen isotopes proposed by Swain and co-workers in 1958: if only zero-point energy matters, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1.44</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>, an exponent fixed by the three masses alone. Exponents clearly away from 1.44, especially for secondary isotope effects, are often taken as evidence of tunnelling and “coupled motion”."},{"h":"How it is used in mechanistic studies"},{"p":"The most common use of an isotope effect is to answer “is C–H bond cleavage rate-determining?”. Simmons and Hartwig stressed that the answer depends on which experiment is done <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-simmons2012\" data-ref=\"simmons2012\">Simmons &amp; Hartwig 2012</a>]</span>: only parallel reactions measuring the rates of H and D substrates separately show, through a large ratio, that C–H cleavage affects the overall rate; intramolecular or intermolecular <b>competition</b> experiments (H and D competing for the same catalyst in one flask) can show large effects but only reveal that C–H cleavage occurs in the selectivity-determining step, not necessarily the rate-determining one. Seen together with the energetic span model of chapter 8 this is clearer still: what sets the rate is a pair of states, not a step."}],"points":[{"h":"Isotopes do not change the surface","p":"In the Born–Oppenheimer approximation only the mass changes; the effect comes from vibrational frequencies.","st":"proved"},{"h":"The semiclassical C–H limit is about 6–7","p":"Losing the whole 2900 cm⁻¹ stretch gives about 6.4 at 25 °C.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Symmetric transition states give the largest effects","p":"Westheimer: with early or late transition states the residual symmetric stretch reduces the effect.","st":"tested"},{"h":"Well above 7 usually means tunnelling","p":"About 80 for soybean lipoxygenase, nearly temperature-independent.","st":"tested"},{"h":"The Swain–Schaad exponent of 1.44","p":"Fixed by the masses of H, D and T; deviations hint at tunnelling or coupled motion.","st":"approx"},{"h":"Competition ≠ parallel experiments","p":"Only parallel rate measurements show whether C–H cleavage controls the overall rate.","st":"tested"}],"math":[{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>ν</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>ν</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi></msub><msqrt><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">X</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi></mrow></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">X</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msqrt></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Frequency scaling in the diatomic approximation."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><msub><mi>k</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi></msub><msub><mi>k</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mtext> ⁣</mtext><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">[</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>h</mi><mi>c</mi><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>ν</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>ν</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mi>T</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">]</mo></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The semiclassical effect when the stretch is lost completely at the transition state."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><msub><mi>k</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi></msub><msub><mi>k</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msubsup><mi>ν</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mo>‡</mo></msubsup><msubsup><mi>ν</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi><mo>‡</mo></msubsup></mfrac><mo>⋅</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>‡</mo></msup></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>⋅</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi></msub><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi></msub></mfrac></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The Bigeleisen–Mayer form: ratio of imaginary frequencies, ratio of reduced partition functions, ratio of tunnelling corrections <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-bigeleisen1947\" data-ref=\"bigeleisen1947\">Bigeleisen &amp; Mayer 1947</a>]</span>."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>k</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi></msub><msub><mi>k</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>1.44</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>k</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi></msub><msub><mi>k</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi></msub></mfrac></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The Swain–Schaad relation (semiclassical, infinitely heavy partner atom)."}],"example":{"q":"A C–H stretch at 2900 cm⁻¹ with carbon mass 12. Estimate the C–D frequency, the zero-point energy difference and the primary isotope effect at 25 °C. What if 30 % of the stretch frequency survives in the transition state?","steps":["Reduced masses: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>12</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1.008</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>13.008</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0.930</mn></mrow></semantics></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>12</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>2.014</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>14.014</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>1.725</mn></mrow></semantics></math>; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>ν</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2900</mn><msqrt><mrow><mn>0.930</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>1.725</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo>=</mo><mn>2129</mn></mrow></semantics></math> cm⁻¹.","Zero-point difference: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2900</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>2129</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>385</mn></mrow></semantics></math> cm⁻¹ = 4.6 kJ/mol.","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1.4388</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>385</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>298.15</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mn>1.86</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>6.4</mn></mrow></semantics></math>.","With 30 % surviving only 70 % is lost; the exponent scales by 0.7 and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mn>1.30</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>3.7</mn></mrow></semantics></math>."],"a":"About 2130 cm⁻¹, 385 cm⁻¹ and 6.4; down to about 3.7 for an asymmetric transition state. Drag “fraction surviving in the transition state” in the demo to see the curve."},"cites":"<a href=\"#ref-bigeleisen1947\" data-ref=\"bigeleisen1947\">Bigeleisen &amp; Mayer 1947</a> · <a href=\"#ref-westheimer1961\" data-ref=\"westheimer1961\">Westheimer 1961</a> · <a href=\"#ref-knapp2002\" data-ref=\"knapp2002\">Knapp et al. 2002</a> · <a href=\"#ref-simmons2012\" data-ref=\"simmons2012\">Simmons &amp; Hartwig 2012</a> · <a href=\"#ref-klinman2013\" data-ref=\"klinman2013\">Klinman &amp; Kohen 2013</a>"},"micro":{"id":"micro","body":[{"p":"An <b>elementary step</b> is a reaction passing a single transition state, and its rate follows the <b>law of mass action</b>: proportional to the product of the participants’ concentrations (or surface coverages, or partial pressures). The rate law of a complex reaction is what the elementary steps add up to, not something written down directly. Empty surface sites <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∗</mo></mrow></semantics></math> count as a “participant” too: Langmuir’s 1918 adsorption isotherm is just the coverage at which the adsorption and desorption steps balance <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-langmuir1918\" data-ref=\"langmuir1918\">Langmuir 1918</a>]</span>. The <a href=\"../chemistry/physical/#c-kinetics\">chemical kinetics</a> and <a href=\"../chemistry/physical/#c-surfaces\">surface chemistry</a> chapters of the Physical Chemistry hall cover the basics."},{"p":"The demo uses the simplest catalytic cycle: A adsorbs, A* turns into B* on the surface, and B desorbs. Each of the three steps has forward and reverse directions, all rate constants come from the energy profile through the Eyring equation, and gas-phase partial pressures multiply the adsorption steps."},{"math":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mtable rowspacing=\"0.25em\" columnalign=\"right left right left\" columnspacing=\"0em 1em 0em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"true\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mo>∗</mo></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"true\"><mrow><mrow></mrow><mo>⇌</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"true\"><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"true\"><mrow><mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></msub><msub><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></msub></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"true\"><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"true\"><mrow><mrow></mrow><mo>⇌</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"true\"><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"true\"><mrow><mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi></msub></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"true\"><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"true\"><mrow><mrow></mrow><mo>⇌</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mo>∗</mo></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"true\"><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"true\"><mrow><mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mi>p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi></msub><msub><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable></semantics></math>","cap":"The rates of the three elementary steps. At steady state r₁ = r₂ = r₃ = TOF, and θ* + θ_A + θ_B = 1."},{"p":"The steady-state condition <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi>θ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></semantics></math> is a set of linear equations (each is first order in the coverages), so it can be solved exactly, with no need for “rate-determining step” or “pre-equilibrium” approximations. The idea is old: for a closed catalytic cycle the steady-state rate can be written as a closed expression in the rate constants. Every drag in the demo re-solves these equations."},{"demo":"micro"},{"h":"The degree of rate control"},{"p":"“Which step is rate-determining?” is often an ill-posed question for multistep reactions. Campbell proposed replacing it with a computable quantity: lower the free energy of one transition state slightly (keeping all equilibrium constants fixed) and see how much the TOF changes <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-campbell2001\" data-ref=\"campbell2001\">Campbell 2001</a>]</span>. Stegelmann, Andreasen and Campbell extended the same definition to intermediates <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-stegelmann2009\" data-ref=\"stegelmann2009\">Stegelmann et al. 2009</a>]</span>:"},{"math":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"3em\" maxsize=\"3em\">∣</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">≠</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi></mrow></mrow></munder><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></munder><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The degree of rate control. The X of the transition states sum to 1 (raising every transition state together multiplies every rate constant by the same factor); those of the intermediates (including the empty site * itself) sum to −1."},{"p":"A transition state with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></semantics></math> is a true “rate-determining” transition state; if several share the total, there is no single rate-determining step. Intermediates have negative <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow></semantics></math>: an over-stable intermediate fills the catalyst (“poisoning”), which the demo shows clearly: as the coverage of A* approaches 1, its <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow></semantics></math> approaches −1. Both sum rules are exact, and the tests check them one by one with finite differences. The same quantity reappears as the “degree of TOF control” in the energetic span model of chapter 8."},{"h":"The Sabatier principle and the volcano plot"},{"p":"A good catalyst binds the reactants “neither too strongly nor too weakly”: too weak and they do not adsorb or activate; too strong and the products cannot leave and the surface clogs. Making this Sabatier principle quantitative takes two empirical relations. One is the <b>linear scaling relations</b>: on a family of surfaces, the binding energies of different adsorbates are linearly related to each other. The other is the <b>BEP relation</b> of chapter 1: the activation energy of a surface reaction is linear in its reaction energy. Nørskov and co-workers found that the BEP relation for a large class of surface dissociation reactions is almost “universal” <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-norskov2002\" data-ref=\"norskov2002\">Nørskov et al. 2002</a>]</span>; Bligaard and co-workers used it in microkinetic models to derive the volcano shape and the location of the optimal binding energy <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-bligaard2004\" data-ref=\"bligaard2004\">Bligaard et al. 2004</a>]</span>."},{"demo":"volcano"},{"p":"Take one or two “descriptors” (say the adsorption energy of some atom) as the horizontal axis, compute every energy from it via the scaling and BEP relations, solve the microkinetics, and you get a map of TOF against the descriptor. That is the skeleton of “descriptor-based catalyst design” <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-medford2015\" data-ref=\"medford2015\">Medford et al. 2015</a>]</span>, and open programs such as CatMAP have made it a standard workflow <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-medford2015c\" data-ref=\"medford2015c\">Medford et al. 2015b</a>]</span>. Its limits are clear too: the scaling relations themselves restrict what can be optimised at the same time (the “curse of scaling relations”), adsorbate–adsorbate interactions change the energies at high coverage, and the active sites of real catalysts are often not ideal facets."},{"note":"<b>Relation to enzyme kinetics and reactors.</b> Applied to an enzyme, the steady state of substrate binding, catalysis and product release is the Michaelis–Menten equation (<a href=\"../chemistry/biochem/#c-enzymes\">the enzymes chapter of the Biochemistry hall</a>). Put the TOF into a mass balance and you have <a href=\"../chemistry/chemeng/#c-reactors\">reactor design in the Chemical Engineering hall</a>.","kind":"info"}],"points":[{"h":"Elementary steps obey mass action","p":"The rate law of a complex reaction is assembled from its elementary steps, not written directly.","st":"proved"},{"h":"The steady state of a cycle can be solved exactly","p":"The equations are linear in the coverages; no rate-determining-step approximation is needed.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Transition-state X values sum to 1","p":"Raising every transition state together scales every rate constant by one factor; this is exact.","st":"proved"},{"h":"The most stable intermediate poisons","p":"An intermediate near full coverage has a degree of rate control near −1.","st":"tested"},{"h":"Volcanoes come from scaling + BEP","p":"Both are empirical relations that work within a family of similar surfaces.","st":"approx"},{"h":"Scaling relations are also a limit","p":"They tie together what could otherwise be tuned independently; breaking them is a research frontier.","st":"frontier"}],"math":[{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>K</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>K</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The Langmuir isotherm <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-langmuir1918\" data-ref=\"langmuir1918\">Langmuir 1918</a>]</span>."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mi>j</mi></munder><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><munder><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></munder><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The steady-state condition and site conservation."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></mrow><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mtext> </mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">)</mo><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">≠</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Campbell’s original definition: change only step i’s rate constant, not its equilibrium constant <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-campbell2001\" data-ref=\"campbell2001\">Campbell 2001</a>]</span>."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>α</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>E</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>β</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msub><mi>E</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>γ</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msub><mi>E</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The BEP relation and a linear scaling relation <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-bligaard2004\" data-ref=\"bligaard2004\">Bligaard et al. 2004</a>]</span>."}],"example":{"q":"The demo’s default settings: 500 K, free energy of A* −30 kJ/mol, transition state for A* → B* at 60 kJ/mol (both relative to A(g) + <i>). The surface is almost full of A</i>. Estimate the TOF. With another catalyst on which A* is only −10 kJ/mol (same transition state), roughly what does the TOF become?","steps":["The surface is almost all A*: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow><mo>≈</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></msub><mo>≈</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mi>T</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>60</mn><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>30</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math>. At 500 K, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mi>T</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.04</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>13</mn></msup><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4.157</mn></mrow></semantics></math> kJ/mol.","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.04</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>13</mn></msup><mo>×</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>90</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>4.157</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>4.1</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>3</mn></msup><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math>; the exact steady state gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4.12</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> with A* coverage 0.998, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.00</mn></mrow></semantics></math> for TS2 and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1.00</mn></mrow></semantics></math> for A*.","With A* at −10 the effective barrier is 70 kJ/mol; if still saturated the TOF would rise by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>20</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>4.157</mn></mrow></msup><mo>≈</mo><mn>120</mn></mrow></semantics></math>. But the A* coverage drops to 0.83, so the exact answer is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4.2</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>5</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math>, about 100 times larger."],"a":"About 4 × 10³ s⁻¹; about 4 × 10⁵ s⁻¹ with weaker binding. Weaken it further and A stops adsorbing, so the TOF falls again: the other side of the volcano."},"cites":"<a href=\"#ref-langmuir1918\" data-ref=\"langmuir1918\">Langmuir 1918</a> · <a href=\"#ref-campbell2001\" data-ref=\"campbell2001\">Campbell 2001</a> · <a href=\"#ref-norskov2002\" data-ref=\"norskov2002\">Nørskov et al. 2002</a> · <a href=\"#ref-bligaard2004\" data-ref=\"bligaard2004\">Bligaard et al. 2004</a> · <a href=\"#ref-stegelmann2009\" data-ref=\"stegelmann2009\">Stegelmann et al. 2009</a> · <a href=\"#ref-medford2015\" data-ref=\"medford2015\">Medford et al. 2015</a> · <a href=\"#ref-medford2015c\" data-ref=\"medford2015c\">Medford et al. 2015b</a>"},"span":{"id":"span","body":[{"p":"Computational studies of catalytic mechanisms often end with an energy profile: the free energies of a sequence of intermediates and transition states. The natural next question is “how many turnovers per second does this catalyst make?”. A common approach is to find “the step with the highest barrier”, call it rate-determining and use the Eyring equation. This can be badly wrong: a very stable intermediate makes later transition states effectively high even if every individual step after it has a modest barrier, because the system has to climb out of that deep valley."},{"p":"For a closed cycle the steady-state TOF can be written exactly in terms of all the rate constants, a result systematised by Christiansen in the 1950s. In 2006 Kozuch and Shaik combined it with the Eyring equation, replacing every rate constant by energies from the profile, to get a formula that depends on the profile alone <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-kozuch2006\" data-ref=\"kozuch2006\">Kozuch &amp; Shaik 2006</a>; <a href=\"#ref-kozuch2011\" data-ref=\"kozuch2011\">Kozuch &amp; Shaik 2011</a>]</span>:"},{"math":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mi>T</mi></mrow><mi>h</mi></mfrac><mtext>  </mtext><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"true\"><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></munder><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></mstyle></mfrac><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi>δ</mi><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">{</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.36em\" columnalign=\"left left\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>0</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The energetic span model. Tᵢ and Iⱼ are the free energies of the transition states and intermediates in the cycle, ΔGr the free energy of the overall reaction (negative if exergonic). Tᵢ lies between Iᵢ and Iᵢ₊₁, so i ≥ j means Tᵢ comes after Iⱼ in the cycle; ΔGr is then subtracted, cancelling the e^{−ΔGr/RT} in the numerator."},{"p":"The terms in the denominator usually differ by orders of magnitude, and the largest one decides almost everything. The pair of states in that term is the TDTS and the TDI, and their energy difference is the <b>energetic span</b>:"},{"math":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">{</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.36em\" columnalign=\"left left\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mi>T</mi></mrow><mi>h</mi></mfrac><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi><mi>E</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The energetic span and the approximate TOF it gives (when the reaction is exergonic enough). i and j number the TDTS and TDI; when the TDTS comes before the TDI (i &lt; j) it is only reached in the next turnover, so the (negative) ΔGr is added."},{"demo":"span"},{"p":"Three conclusions follow. First, <b>states, not steps, set the rate</b>: the TDTS and TDI need not be adjacent and can be several steps apart <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-kozuch2011b\" data-ref=\"kozuch2011b\">Kozuch &amp; Martin 2011</a>]</span>. Second, ΔGr matters: the more exergonic the reaction, the smaller the spans of the combinations in which the TDTS precedes the TDI; as the reaction approaches equilibrium (ΔGr → 0) the TOF goes to zero. Third, “lowering the highest barrier” need not help: if the TDI is a very stable intermediate, destabilising it is often more effective than lowering any transition state."},{"h":"The degree of TOF control"},{"p":"Using the same idea as in chapter 7, Kozuch and Shaik defined each state’s <b>degree of TOF control</b>: shift its energy slightly and see how much the logarithm of the TOF changes. Transition states sum to 1 and intermediates to −1; in most realistic profiles the TDTS has nearly 1, the TDI nearly −1 and the rest almost 0 <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-kozuch2011\" data-ref=\"kozuch2011\">Kozuch &amp; Shaik 2011</a>]</span>. It is the same quantity as Campbell’s degree of rate control <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-stegelmann2009\" data-ref=\"stegelmann2009\">Stegelmann et al. 2009</a>]</span>, one starting from the language of catalyst surfaces and the other from the energy profiles of homogeneous catalysis. The bar chart in the demo shows it."},{"h":"Where it applies"},{"list":["It assumes the cycle is at <b>steady state</b> and every step is first or pseudo-first order (substrate and product concentrations are folded into the profile through <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></semantics></math> corrections).","A single cycle with no branches. Competing cycles or catalyst deactivation need full microkinetics (chapter 7).","Every rate constant follows the Eyring equation with unit transmission coefficient; tunnelling needs separate corrections (chapter 5).","The largest error is not the model but the energies: DFT free energies carry errors of about 10 kJ/mol, roughly a factor of 50 in TOF. It is therefore best for comparing similar catalysts, not for predicting absolute TOFs."]},{"note":"<b>How it ties to the other chapters.</b> Each point on the profile is a stationary point from chapters 1 and 2; each arrow’s rate constant is the Eyring equation of chapter 3; and “a pair of states sets the rate” explains why the isotope effects of chapter 6 sometimes appear and sometimes vanish: a primary effect is seen only when the labelled bond breaks in the TDTS and the experiment measures parallel rates.","kind":"info"}],"points":[{"h":"It is a rewriting of the exact steady state","p":"The full denominator is Christiansen’s formula; in the demo it matches the solution of the linear equations to the last digit.","st":"proved"},{"h":"TOF ≈ (k_BT/h) e^{−δE/RT}","p":"Keep only the largest denominator term; accurate when the reaction is exergonic enough.","st":"approx"},{"h":"The TDTS and TDI need not be adjacent","p":"A pair of states sets the rate; the notion of a “rate-determining step” is no longer needed.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Add ΔGr when the TDTS precedes the TDI","p":"That transition state is reached only in the next turnover; the more exergonic, the smaller such spans.","st":"proved"},{"h":"TOF → 0 as ΔGr → 0","p":"The numerator e^{−ΔGr/RT} − 1 goes to zero: near equilibrium net turnover stops.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Best for comparisons, not absolute predictions","p":"A 10 kJ/mol error in energy is roughly a factor of 50 in TOF.","st":"tested"}],"math":[{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mi>T</mi></mrow><mi>h</mi></mfrac><mtext> </mtext><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></munder><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The full energetic span formula <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-kozuch2006\" data-ref=\"kozuch2006\">Kozuch &amp; Shaik 2006</a>]</span>."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></msub><mtext>  </mtext><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mo>+</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The energetic span; the bracketed ΔGr is added only when the TDTS precedes the TDI <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-kozuch2011\" data-ref=\"kozuch2011\">Kozuch &amp; Shaik 2011</a>]</span>."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi></mrow></munder><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"/><munder><mo>∑</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></munder><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The degree of TOF control and its two sum rules."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi><mo lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">+</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mi>T</mi></mrow><mi>h</mi></mfrac><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi><mo lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">−</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mi>T</mi></mrow><mi>h</mi></mfrac><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Forward and reverse rate constants of each step on the profile (Iₙ = I₀ + ΔGr)."}],"example":{"q":"A three-step cycle (25 °C): intermediates <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>30</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>10</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>, transition states <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>50</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>25</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>45</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math> (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> between <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math>), <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>40</mn></mrow></semantics></math> kJ/mol. What is the largest single-step barrier? The energetic span? The TOF?","steps":["Single-step barriers: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>50</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>50</mn></mrow></semantics></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>25</mn><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>30</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>55</mn></mrow></semantics></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>45</mn><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>10</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>55</mn></mrow></semantics></math>. The largest is 55 kJ/mol, which would suggest TOF <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>≈</mo><mn>6.21</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>12</mn></msup><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>55</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2.479</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1.4</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>3</mn></msup><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math>.","The most stable intermediate is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>30</mn></mrow></semantics></math> and the highest transition state <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>45</mn></mrow></semantics></math>, which comes after <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math>: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>45</mn><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>30</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>75</mn></mrow></semantics></math> kJ/mol. Other combinations, such as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math> before <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, give <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>50</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>30</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>40</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>40</mn></mrow></semantics></math>, much smaller.","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mn>6.21</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>12</mn></msup><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>75</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2.479</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0.45</mn><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math>; the full formula and the exact steady state both give <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.450</mn><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math>."],"a":"The largest single-step barrier of 55 kJ/mol would overestimate the TOF about 3000-fold; the real span is 75 kJ/mol and TOF ≈ 0.45 s⁻¹. The “trap intermediate” preset in the demo is this profile."},"cites":"<a href=\"#ref-kozuch2006\" data-ref=\"kozuch2006\">Kozuch &amp; Shaik 2006</a> · <a href=\"#ref-stegelmann2009\" data-ref=\"stegelmann2009\">Stegelmann et al. 2009</a> · <a href=\"#ref-kozuch2011\" data-ref=\"kozuch2011\">Kozuch &amp; Shaik 2011</a> · <a href=\"#ref-kozuch2011b\" data-ref=\"kozuch2011b\">Kozuch &amp; Martin 2011</a>"}}};
